WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:10.050
<font color="#0080FF"><b>Improved & Synced by BanglarBagh</b></font>

2
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.680
<b><font color="#408080">FRANK:</font></b> Hey! Come on.
Let's move!

3
00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:54.807
<b><font color="#408080">MARY:</font></b> No!

4
00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:56.450
<b><font color="#408080">FRANK:</font></b> Let me see.
<b><font color="#408080">MARY:</font></b> No!

5
00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.250
Come on. I made you
a special breakfast.

6
00:00:58.400 --> 00:00:59.561
<b><font color="#408080">MARY:</font></b> You can't cook.

7
00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:02.246
Hey, Mary, open up!

8
00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:08.931
You look beautiful.

9
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.804
I look like a Disney character.

10
00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:15.961
<b><font color="#808000">(SIGHS)</font></b>

11
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.004
Where's the "special"?

12
00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:23.161
The what?

13
00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.851
You said you made me
a special breakfast.

14
00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:32.964
Please don't make me go.

15
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.282
You can keep homeschooling me.

16
00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:36.856
I've taught you
everything I know.

17
00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.086
But I don't wanna go.

18
00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.408
Well, I don't wanna go
to work, but I do.

19
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:44.449
You don't go to work.
You fix the boats on a dock.

20
00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:47.561
Okay. It's a poor example,
but you're still going.

21
00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:51.320
But what about Fred?

22
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:52.447
<b><font color="#408080">FRANK:</font></b> What about him?

23
00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:53.656
<b><font color="#408080">MARY:</font></b> You won't
take care of him.

24
00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:54.761
You don't like cats.

25
00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:55.976
I don't like two-eyed cats.

26
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.256
Fred, as you know, is monocular.

27
00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:58.884
Who's gonna throw him
his ping pong ball?

28
00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:00.800
Fred's gonna be fine.
No more argument, okay?

29
00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:02.405
We've discussed this ad nauseam.

30
00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:06.921
What's ad nauseam?

31
00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:08.320
Oh, you don't know?

32
00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:09.920
Well, looks like
someone needs school.

33
00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.602
<b><font color="#808000">(CHILDREN CHATTERING)</font></b>

34
00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:20.045
This is gonna be fun.

35
00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:21.360
You're gonna meet kids today

36
00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:23.607
you can borrow money from
the rest of your life.

37
00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.121
Come on.

38
00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:32.771
You're gonna be great.

39
00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:35.606
You know, just...

40
00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:38.970
I don't know. Try bein' a kid.

41
00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:54.650
<b><font color="#808000">(KNOCKING)</font></b>

42
00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:58.087
<b><font color="#408080">ROBERTA:</font></b> Mmm-mmm.

43
00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:02.967
Frank!

44
00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:06.885
Frank, I know you hear me.

45
00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:11.926
Frank!

46
00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:16.085
<b><font color="#808000">(LOCKS DOOR)</font></b>

47
00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:22.881
<b><font color="#808000">(DOORKNOB RATTLING)</font></b>

48
00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:31.971
There's still time
for you to undo this nonsense.

49
00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:34.521
Go get in your car
and go get that child.

50
00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:36.966
Are you technically allowed to

51
00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:38.485
use those keys
whenever you want?

52
00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:40.244
How can you stand there...

53
00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:41.656
acting all calm
and everything, Frank,

54
00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:42.761
and make light of this?

55
00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.603
Now go get her back
before it's too late.

56
00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:47.856
She gotta get out in the world.

57
00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:48.960
She has no friends her age.

58
00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:50.445
No social skills.

59
00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:53.487
She doesn't know
how to be a kid.

60
00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:56.051
Two nights ago, she told me

61
00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:57.536
that even if Germany
bails out the euro...

62
00:03:57.560 --> 00:03:59.881
there could still be
a worldwide depression.

63
00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:02.680
I was staring at the ceiling
for three hours.

64
00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:06.401
<b><font color="#808000">(CRYING)</font></b> I'm so worried.

65
00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:10.282
<b><font color="#808000">(SIGHS)</font></b> Come on, Roberta.

66
00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:11.566
If you start crying,

67
00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:13.280
I'm gonna have to
pretend to start crying.

68
00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:15.766
Hey.

69
00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:18.362
You know, there's something
you could be overlookin'.

70
00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:20.326
This could work out.

71
00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:23.007
Maybe.

72
00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:26.002
But if anybody takes
that baby away...

73
00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:28.441
I'll smother you in your sleep.

74
00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:30.405
- Morning, Fred.
- <b><font color="#808000">(KEYS JANGLING)</font></b>

75
00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:32.761
<b><font color="#808000">(DOOR CLOSES)</font></b>

76
00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:36.280
<b><font color="#808000">(SIGHS)</font></b>

77
00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:38.728
<b><font color="#408080">BONNIE:</font></b> One plus one is two.

78
00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:40.843
That is fact number one
of today.

79
00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:44.368
Who can tell me
what two plus two is?

80
00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:45.726
Who's got it?

81
00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:47.926
- Mmm-hmm?
- Four.

82
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.001
Excellent, Donna.

83
00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:52.004
It is. Two plus two is,
indeed, four.

84
00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:55.446
Okay, who wants to try
three plus three?

85
00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:00.050
Three plus three? Really?

86
00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:02.881
I beg your pardon?

87
00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:05.523
What kinda school
is this anyway?

88
00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:07.330
It's the kind of school

89
00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:09.607
where students don't speak
without permission.

90
00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:12.525
All right.
But everyone knows it's six.

91
00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:16.130
Nobody in this classroom
speaks...

92
00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:18.681
unless they are called upon.

93
00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:20.962
Okay? ls everybody clear
on that?

94
00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:22.929
<b><font color="#408080">STUDENTS:</font></b> Yes, ma'am.

95
00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:25.606
Good. Mary, can you
stand up please?

96
00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:28.523
Stand on up. Stand up, babe.

97
00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:31.566
These questions are for you
because you're so advanced.

98
00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:35.964
What is nine plus eight?

99
00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:37.451
17.

100
00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:40.690
Yeah. Yes, it is.
That's good.

101
00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:42.960
What is...

102
00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:44.360
15 plus 17?

103
00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:45.965
32.

104
00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:50.685
<b><font color="#808000">(STAMMERS)</font></b>
Yeah. That is right.

105
00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:52.401
All right, then.

106
00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:56.451
Well, what is 57 plus 135?

107
00:05:57.080 --> 00:05:59.287
192.

108
00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:04.401
Yeah.

109
00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:13.321
Can you tell me...

110
00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:19.123
what 57 multiplied by 135 is?

111
00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:21.530
<b><font color="#808000">(EXHALES)</font></b>

112
00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:32.647
Okay.

113
00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:36.929
Who can tell me what four...

114
00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:41.205
7,695.

115
00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:43.604
<b><font color="#408080">GIRL:</font></b> I don't think
anyone can do that.

116
00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:48.761
<b><font color="#408080">MARY:</font></b> The square root is 87.7.

117
00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:51.806
And change.

118
00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:55.809
Now what does ad nauseam mean?

119
00:06:57.880 --> 00:06:59.609
I have a very strange kid.

120
00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:01.250
I have 27 strange kids.

121
00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:02.487
Good morning.

122
00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:03.936
Good morning, good morning,
good morning.

123
00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:05.976
<b><font color="#408080">BONNIE:</font></b> That's great, guys,
but take your seats, okay?

124
00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:08.241
Oh, God. See ya.

125
00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:10.406
Good morning, Ms. Stevenson.

126
00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:11.686
Good morning.

127
00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:13.569
Mind if I sit in?

128
00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:14.721
Not at all!

129
00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:16.370
Okay, class.

130
00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:17.521
I have a treat for you.

131
00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:22.970
I would like to introduce you
to our principal, Mrs. Davis.

132
00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:24.963
Good morning, first graders.

133
00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:28.249
<b><font color="#408080">STUDENTS:</font></b>
Good morning, Mrs. Davis.

134
00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:31.006
Are you ready for a great year?

135
00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:32.685
<b><font color="#408080">STUDENTS:</font></b> Yes!

136
00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:35.525
Uh... Yes, Mary?

137
00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:36.881
She's the boss?

138
00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:40.331
Mrs. Davis is our principal.
Mm-kay...

139
00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:41.806
Okay. Now I want you

140
00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:43.296
to get on your phone
and call Frank...

141
00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:45.243
and tell him to
get me out of here!

142
00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:52.406
<b><font color="#408080">AMANDA:</font></b> Wait a minute.
That's the dad? I know him.

143
00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:54.642
I see him at Ferg's
almost every Friday night.

144
00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:57.122
He's the quiet, damaged hot guy.

145
00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:01.006
What are you doing at Ferg's
every Friday night?

146
00:08:01.160 --> 00:08:03.128
Trying to get picked up by him.

147
00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:10.884
Excuse me? Hi.

148
00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:13.442
Oh. Look, it's my teacher.

149
00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:16.331
Probably wants to remind me
what one plus one is.

150
00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:17.447
Go to the car, okay?

151
00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:19.846
- Hi.
- Hi.

152
00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:21.536
Sorry to yell at you
and then chase you down.

153
00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:22.641
It's okay. Mary's teacher?

154
00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:24.006
Yes. I'm Bonnie Stevenson.

155
00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:25.640
Frank. How are you?
Sorry about today.

156
00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:27.171
I know she got
a little overexcited.

157
00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:28.456
I think
it's just first day jitters.

158
00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:29.527
Sure. Yeah.

159
00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:31.256
We're running a little late
actually. We gotta get going.

160
00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:33.567
Okay. I don't even wanna
talk about that.

161
00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:35.324
- I'll keep you just a minute.
- Okay.

162
00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:38.327
I think your daughter...

163
00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:40.801
I think Mary might be gifted.

164
00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:43.001
What?

165
00:08:43.160 --> 00:08:47.165
Yeah. Today in Math,
she answered some really...

166
00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:49.084
- Oh, no, no, no. That's...
- No, please.

167
00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:50.416
- It's not gifted.
- Really difficult questions.

168
00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:51.487
All right.

169
00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:52.856
Just that
a seven-year-old would...

170
00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:54.086
It's Trachtenberg.

171
00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:57.128
- Jakow Trachtenberg.
- I'm sorry?

172
00:08:57.280 --> 00:08:59.487
Spent seven years
in a Nazi concentration camp.

173
00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:02.524
Developed a system
to rapidly solve problems.

174
00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:04.805
It's the Trachtenberg method.

175
00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:07.326
But she's...

176
00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:09.882
I mean, she's seven though.

177
00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:11.451
I learned it when I was eight.

178
00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:13.405
Do I look gifted to you?

179
00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:16.360
It's kind of gone out of vogue

180
00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:18.647
since the invention
of the calculator...

181
00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:21.207
but, uh, I can still win
a drink at a bar using it.

182
00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:22.841
Sorry for today.

183
00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:25.163
- Won't happen again.
- Okay.

184
00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:27.368
Nice to meet you. Bonnie.

185
00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:28.521
Frank.

186
00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:30.125
- <b><font color="#808000">(CAR DOOR CLOSES)</font></b>
- <b><font color="#808000">(ENGINE STARTS)</font></b>

187
00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:51.206
Hmm.

188
00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:53.096
For the record,
I didn't wanna go

189
00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:54.724
to the stupid school
in the first place.

190
00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:56.291
And the boy in the next row

191
00:09:56.360 --> 00:09:59.045
acts inappropriately
for someone who's a child.

192
00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:02.324
I'm sorry. I'm still passive
aggressively ignoring you.

193
00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:05.691
Other kids answer questions.
They don't get into trouble.

194
00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:08.002
You didn't get in trouble
for answering questions.

195
00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:09.525
You yelled at the principal.

196
00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:12.841
You know what? You're gonna
find this interesting.

197
00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:15.281
So, I googled "first graders
who yell at the principal..."

198
00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:16.736
and statistically,
you're never gonna

199
00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:18.125
believe how many kids do it.

200
00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:19.287
How many?

201
00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:20.641
None.

202
00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:22.882
<b><font color="#808000">(STARTS MOTOR)</font></b>

203
00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:27.085
Frank, I'm sorry.
I'm really sorry.

204
00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:28.480
Yeah, right.

205
00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:31.288
You can't show off
like that at school.

206
00:10:31.480 --> 00:10:32.641
I know.

207
00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:34.680
You promised you wouldn't,
then the first day...

208
00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:36.768
I know, I know. I screwed up.

209
00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:44.325
Do you think maybe
this boat needs a test ride?

210
00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:48.407
Go get Fred.

211
00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:51.091
Don't run.

212
00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:55.488
<b><font color="#808000">(music playing)</font></b>

213
00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:06.642
<i><font color="#D900D9">♪</font> I listen to the wind
To the wind of my soul <font color="#D900D9">♪</font></i>

214
00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:12.204
<i><font color="#D900D9">♪</font> Where I'll end up Well, I think <font color="#D900D9">♪</font></i>

215
00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:15.841
<i><font color="#D900D9">♪</font> Only God really knows <font color="#D900D9">♪</font></i>

216
00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:21.964
<i><font color="#D900D9">♪</font> I've sat upon the setting sun <font color="#D900D9">♪</font></i>

217
00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:27.131
<i><font color="#D900D9">♪</font> But never, never, never, never <font color="#D900D9">♪</font></i>

218
00:11:27.960 --> 00:11:31.169
<i><font color="#D900D9">♪</font> I never wanted water once <font color="#D900D9">♪</font></i>

219
00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:35.089
<i><font color="#D900D9">♪</font> No, never, never, never <font color="#D900D9">♪</font></i>

220
00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:37.811
Fred loves to watch
the sandpipers.

221
00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:40.729
He thinks he'd like to
catch one...

222
00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:42.484
but he'd regret it.

223
00:11:43.400 --> 00:11:45.323
Fred's not a killer.

224
00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:46.970
He's a lover.

225
00:11:48.280 --> 00:11:49.850
<b><font color="#808000">(MARY SIGHS)</font></b>

226
00:11:53.720 --> 00:11:56.644
Would my mom have wanted me
to go to this school?

227
00:11:57.040 --> 00:11:58.485
I can only guess.

228
00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:00.921
But I'll tell you what,

229
00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:02.729
she would have wanted you
to have friends.

230
00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:04.724
Idiot friends?

231
00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:08.840
And she'd want you to have
compassion for others.

232
00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:12.891
Like a cat can have
for a sandpiper.

233
00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:17.962
But what if they don't like me?

234
00:12:20.080 --> 00:12:21.286
Then they're idiots.

235
00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:34.280
Good morning, class.

236
00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:38.166
<b><font color="#408080">STUDENTS:</font></b>
Good morning, Ms. Stevenson.

237
00:12:39.040 --> 00:12:40.166
Good morning, Mary.

238
00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:46.161
<b><font color="#808000">(SING-SONG)</font></b>
Good morning, Ms. Stevenson.

239
00:12:46.960 --> 00:12:48.041
Okay, good morning.

240
00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:53.971
Get a hold of yourself.

241
00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:58.447
For my Show and Tell, I have...

242
00:12:58.600 --> 00:13:00.921
the most awesome cat
in the history of time.

243
00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:04.529
Not only
is his name Fred, but...

244
00:13:06.200 --> 00:13:07.690
<b><font color="#808000">(FRED MEOWS)</font></b>

245
00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:11.204
He has just one eye.

246
00:13:11.440 --> 00:13:13.408
Uno. Go ahead and count...

247
00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:16.763
but I promise you,
it's always one.

248
00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:18.445
How did he lose his eye?

249
00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:21.847
I don't know. I wasn't there.

250
00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:23.445
Just found him
inside of a trash can

251
00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:26.524
next to a bottle of alcohol
or something.

252
00:13:26.680 --> 00:13:29.889
He is a smart, smart cat,
but no one realizes that.

253
00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:33.684
No one understands him.
No one.

254
00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:37.607
<b><font color="#408080">BONNIE:</font></b> Put your name
on the top of the paper...

255
00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:40.730
so that I know
who did such good work.

256
00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:42.120
<b><font color="#408080">BOY:</font></b> I left my pen with you.

257
00:13:42.200 --> 00:13:43.611
<b><font color="#408080">BONNIE:</font></b>
Pass those around, guys.

258
00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:45.290
Okay? I wanna see your work.

259
00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:47.488
Turn around, face the desk.

260
00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:50.288
Think about
how you get to the answer.

261
00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:54.286
It's not just about,
you know, guessing.

262
00:13:54.440 --> 00:13:55.851
Don't be messy.

263
00:13:56.760 --> 00:13:57.807
Done.

264
00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:03.881
<b><font color="#808000">(TAPPING PEN ON DESK)</font></b>

265
00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:09.526
Let's see.

266
00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:13.171
Mmm-hmm.

267
00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:16.130
Okay. I thought
you might finish quickly,

268
00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:17.690
so a little something extra.

269
00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:08.521
Done.

270
00:15:23.160 --> 00:15:24.844
<b><font color="#808000">(TYPING)</font></b>

271
00:15:54.320 --> 00:15:56.448
<b><font color="#808000">(GASPS)</font></b>

272
00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:05.206
Oh...

273
00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:28.807
<b><font color="#808000">(UPBEAT MUSIC PLAYING)</font></b>

274
00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:31.327
<b><font color="#808000">(INDISTINCT CHATTERING)</font></b>

275
00:16:45.200 --> 00:16:46.326
Do you know who I am?

276
00:16:46.520 --> 00:16:49.967
Hey! Mary's teacher...

277
00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:52.241
BOTH: Bonnie.

278
00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:54.004
You lied to me.

279
00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:56.086
Okay.

280
00:16:56.280 --> 00:16:58.123
- Can you narrow it down?
- Mmm-hmm.

281
00:16:58.280 --> 00:17:01.523
I'd like to know the truth
about Mary, my student...

282
00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:03.682
your niece.

283
00:17:07.120 --> 00:17:08.326
<b><font color="#408080">FRANK:</font></b> I was running

284
00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:09.686
ridiculously late for a date...

285
00:17:09.880 --> 00:17:11.928
and my sister showed up
with Mary.

286
00:17:12.080 --> 00:17:13.764
And she never just
showed up unannounced.

287
00:17:13.840 --> 00:17:15.365
She never showed up
with the baby.

288
00:17:15.960 --> 00:17:19.203
She said she wanted to talk.

289
00:17:20.440 --> 00:17:22.010
She said she needed to talk.

290
00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:24.927
I told her I would talk to her
when I got back.

291
00:17:25.080 --> 00:17:27.120
I think I figured showing up
that late to my date...

292
00:17:27.240 --> 00:17:29.049
would hurt my chances
of gettin' laid.

293
00:17:31.400 --> 00:17:33.687
Came home that night
and Mary was on the couch...

294
00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:36.525
and found Diane
on the bathroom floor.

295
00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:40.968
I mean, there's no way
I coulda known...

296
00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:43.604
but I shoulda known.

297
00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:46.486
Six and a half years later,

298
00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:48.085
me and Mary
are still hangin' out.

299
00:17:48.720 --> 00:17:50.688
And what about the father?

300
00:17:50.880 --> 00:17:52.166
<b><font color="#808000">(SCOFFS)</font></b>

301
00:17:52.600 --> 00:17:54.284
Diane was so socially awkward...

302
00:17:54.440 --> 00:17:57.171
she didn't date enough to know
a good guy from a bad guy.

303
00:17:57.320 --> 00:17:59.800
This particular guy
didn't last a month.

304
00:17:59.960 --> 00:18:01.644
And no grandparents?

305
00:18:02.920 --> 00:18:07.767
Dad died when I was eight.
My mom... <b><font color="#808000">(SCOFFS)</font></b>

306
00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:09.969
Short version is
she turned her back on Diane

307
00:18:10.040 --> 00:18:11.246
when she got pregnant.

308
00:18:13.120 --> 00:18:14.121
Why?

309
00:18:16.520 --> 00:18:18.204
Didn't fit into her plan.

310
00:18:19.480 --> 00:18:22.131
She's an exacting woman,
my mother.

311
00:18:22.280 --> 00:18:24.089
Uncompromising.

312
00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:27.206
Very British.

313
00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:30.125
Do you mean,
British in demeanor or...

314
00:18:30.280 --> 00:18:32.362
- No. Like she's...
- She's from England.

315
00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:34.204
From England. Yeah.

316
00:18:36.560 --> 00:18:38.085
<b><font color="#808000">(SIGHS)</font></b>

317
00:18:39.440 --> 00:18:42.444
So, Mary is a genius?

318
00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:49.329
<b><font color="#808000">(SIGHS)</font></b>

319
00:18:50.480 --> 00:18:52.608
<b><font color="#808000">(CHILDREN CHATTERING)</font></b>

320
00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:16.451
ALL: Oh!

321
00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:17.607
<b><font color="#808000">(BOY LAUGHING)</font></b>

322
00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:19.125
Look out. Look out.

323
00:19:19.280 --> 00:19:22.045
Your little animals
are escaping, dumbass.

324
00:19:22.200 --> 00:19:25.124
Hey! You should not
be laughing!

325
00:19:25.960 --> 00:19:28.361
You did a mean thing
to a little kid!

326
00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:30.085
What are you gonna do about it?

327
00:19:35.280 --> 00:19:36.327
<b><font color="#808000">(SMACKS)</font></b>

328
00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:41.401
<b><font color="#808000">(CRYING)</font></b>

329
00:19:50.440 --> 00:19:53.171
Hey-Hey, you okay?

330
00:19:53.880 --> 00:19:56.724
You good? All right.

331
00:19:58.240 --> 00:19:59.810
How do we resolve this?

332
00:19:59.880 --> 00:20:01.484
You know, she could be expelled.

333
00:20:02.080 --> 00:20:04.367
God. I hope not.

334
00:20:04.520 --> 00:20:06.727
But if every other first-time
offender was expelled,

335
00:20:06.800 --> 00:20:07.881
I guess fair is fair.

336
00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:09.724
Are you gonna
take this seriously?

337
00:20:09.880 --> 00:20:13.487
Sure. You don't bluff me,
I don't bluff you.

338
00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:17.649
Ms. Stevenson believes

339
00:20:17.720 --> 00:20:19.688
that your child
is exceptional...

340
00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:22.446
and has talents
that our curriculum

341
00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:24.045
can't begin to challenge.

342
00:20:24.240 --> 00:20:26.004
It just so happens

343
00:20:26.120 --> 00:20:28.248
that I am good friends
with the headmaster

344
00:20:28.400 --> 00:20:31.131
of the Oaks Academy
for Gifted Education.

345
00:20:31.280 --> 00:20:32.520
He has always said

346
00:20:32.600 --> 00:20:34.568
that if I find
that one in a million...

347
00:20:34.720 --> 00:20:37.724
And the one in a million
has a $30,000 tuition.

348
00:20:38.400 --> 00:20:40.528
Mr. Adler, I can get
your daughter a scholarship.

349
00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:41.761
Full ride.

350
00:20:42.080 --> 00:20:43.923
I wouldn't say it
if I couldn't do it.

351
00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:47.889
<b><font color="#808000">(EXHALES)</font></b>

352
00:20:56.560 --> 00:20:59.723
I realize, putting that girl
in that setting,

353
00:20:59.880 --> 00:21:02.929
you know, 99 times out of
a 100, that's what you do.

354
00:21:03.080 --> 00:21:04.445
It's the Oaks.
It's a great school.

355
00:21:04.520 --> 00:21:05.681
I looked into it.

356
00:21:06.560 --> 00:21:08.847
But this family has a history
with those schools.

357
00:21:10.440 --> 00:21:12.727
And I think the last thing
that little girl needs

358
00:21:12.880 --> 00:21:15.326
is reinforcement
that she's different.

359
00:21:16.280 --> 00:21:18.282
Trust me, she knows. So...

360
00:21:20.560 --> 00:21:24.246
I think Mary,
I think she's gotta be here.

361
00:21:25.760 --> 00:21:27.683
Today's a bad ending.
You can't hit people.

362
00:21:28.200 --> 00:21:29.964
But a 12-year-old
bullies a seven-year-old

363
00:21:30.040 --> 00:21:31.371
and she stands up?

364
00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:34.136
Do you know how important
it is to me that she did that?

365
00:21:34.160 --> 00:21:36.367
You know how proud I am
of her? Aren't you?

366
00:21:36.560 --> 00:21:38.847
Mr. Adler, your daughter
shattered a young boy's...

367
00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:41.207
I know. You can't hit people.

368
00:21:41.400 --> 00:21:43.323
That will be made very clear.
I get that.

369
00:21:43.480 --> 00:21:46.131
But Mrs. Davis,
if we separate our leaders,

370
00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:49.767
if we segregate them
from people like you and me,

371
00:21:49.920 --> 00:21:51.922
you get congressmen.

372
00:21:53.520 --> 00:21:54.521
So, I'm sorry.

373
00:21:54.600 --> 00:21:56.489
I wish I could take
your offer, but Mary stays.

374
00:21:56.640 --> 00:21:57.721
Unless you kick her out.

375
00:21:57.920 --> 00:22:00.730
This is a mistake. We'll never
be able to raise this child

376
00:22:00.920 --> 00:22:03.526
to the level of scholarship
she deserves.

377
00:22:04.920 --> 00:22:08.811
Well, just dumb her down
into a decent human being.

378
00:22:09.320 --> 00:22:10.401
Everybody wins.

379
00:22:12.640 --> 00:22:13.766
<b><font color="#808000">(DOOR OPENS)</font></b>

380
00:22:15.560 --> 00:22:18.609
You know what?
Can you excuse me one minute?

381
00:22:19.560 --> 00:22:21.289
Bring me
every piece of paperwork

382
00:22:21.400 --> 00:22:22.765
we have on this girl.

383
00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:25.961
Hey.

384
00:22:29.320 --> 00:22:31.527
Are you sure
you know what you're doing?

385
00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:36.521
No.

386
00:22:42.760 --> 00:22:43.921
Okay. Mary, I think

387
00:22:44.000 --> 00:22:45.656
you have something you'd like
to say to the class.

388
00:22:45.680 --> 00:22:47.921
You wanna hop up and say it?

389
00:22:53.640 --> 00:22:56.644
Okay. I'm supposed to
say something.

390
00:22:57.080 --> 00:23:01.165
Hitting people is wrong
even if they're bad.

391
00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:05.211
I won't do it again,
so don't be afraid of me.

392
00:23:07.640 --> 00:23:09.768
Okay. Thank you.

393
00:23:09.920 --> 00:23:12.366
Now can I say something
I wanna say?

394
00:23:13.120 --> 00:23:14.121
Sure.

395
00:23:15.520 --> 00:23:17.170
Before they ruined it,

396
00:23:17.320 --> 00:23:19.891
Justin's zoo
was the best art project.

397
00:23:21.360 --> 00:23:24.091
By far. It was awesome.

398
00:23:25.440 --> 00:23:26.441
<b><font color="#808000">(SCATTERED APPLAUSE)</font></b>

399
00:23:26.600 --> 00:23:29.843
Come on. A little harder.

400
00:23:30.040 --> 00:23:31.769
<b><font color="#808000">(ALL APPLAUDING)</font></b>

401
00:23:32.000 --> 00:23:33.047
Okay.

402
00:23:34.480 --> 00:23:37.370
Thank you. Thank you.

403
00:23:43.720 --> 00:23:46.121
There's a lady standing
in front of our door.

404
00:23:46.320 --> 00:23:47.367
Who is it?

405
00:23:47.520 --> 00:23:49.727
How should I know? I'm seven.

406
00:23:52.280 --> 00:23:53.281
<b><font color="#808000">(STOPS ENGINE)</font></b>

407
00:23:53.680 --> 00:23:55.842
That would be your grandmother.

408
00:23:56.560 --> 00:23:59.325
Holy shit!

409
00:24:01.880 --> 00:24:04.201
An Apple? Whoa.

410
00:24:05.600 --> 00:24:07.443
It's a MacBook, darling.

411
00:24:07.600 --> 00:24:10.001
Top of the line
with the Retina Display.

412
00:24:10.160 --> 00:24:13.004
Hey, you know who else
has a Retina Display?

413
00:24:13.080 --> 00:24:14.127
Fred!

414
00:24:14.800 --> 00:24:18.168
Mary, I understand
you like mathematics.

415
00:24:18.320 --> 00:24:20.129
So, on there,

416
00:24:20.280 --> 00:24:21.361
you'll find a great

417
00:24:21.480 --> 00:24:23.926
out-of-print book
by Charles Zimmer

418
00:24:24.200 --> 00:24:26.680
<i>called Transitions
in Advanced Algebra.</i>

419
00:24:27.280 --> 00:24:29.442
Yeah. Love that book.

420
00:24:31.240 --> 00:24:32.526
You're saying you've read it?

421
00:24:32.680 --> 00:24:35.809
Yeah. I've kind of moved on
to differential equations now.

422
00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:39.128
Don't forget your manners.
Thank your grandma.

423
00:24:39.280 --> 00:24:40.520
Thank you, Grandma.

424
00:24:40.680 --> 00:24:43.411
Grandmother or Evelyn
will do just fine.

425
00:24:44.680 --> 00:24:47.365
There's so much more on there.

426
00:24:47.520 --> 00:24:49.284
Things I know you'll find
really challenging.

427
00:24:49.440 --> 00:24:51.044
Yes. But sadly,
it's a school night

428
00:24:51.120 --> 00:24:52.451
and there's homework to do.

429
00:24:52.600 --> 00:24:54.967
What a surprise though, right?

430
00:24:55.120 --> 00:24:56.963
Say good night
to Grandmother or Evelyn.

431
00:24:57.760 --> 00:24:59.125
Good night.

432
00:24:59.680 --> 00:25:01.728
I'd kill a priest
for a Benadryl.

433
00:25:01.880 --> 00:25:03.211
Still with the allergies?

434
00:25:03.360 --> 00:25:05.203
Why in God's name
have you got a cat?

435
00:25:05.280 --> 00:25:06.441
You don't even like cats.

436
00:25:06.600 --> 00:25:08.204
It's not my cat.
It's Mary's cat.

437
00:25:08.280 --> 00:25:09.691
I'm just along for the ride.

438
00:25:09.760 --> 00:25:10.841
So let me guess.

439
00:25:10.920 --> 00:25:12.570
Our lovely principal,
Ms. Davis.

440
00:25:12.720 --> 00:25:14.961
Never get on the bad side
of small-minded people

441
00:25:15.120 --> 00:25:16.485
who have a little authority.

442
00:25:16.640 --> 00:25:18.290
I thought I taught you that.

443
00:25:19.880 --> 00:25:21.041
What are you doin' here?

444
00:25:21.120 --> 00:25:22.256
You don't think I have the right

445
00:25:22.280 --> 00:25:23.327
to see my granddaughter?

446
00:25:23.400 --> 00:25:25.767
I do. I'm thrilled your
seven-year exhaustive search

447
00:25:25.960 --> 00:25:28.327
has finally come
to a fruitful conclusion.

448
00:25:29.160 --> 00:25:32.130
I don't think this is
an appropriate time to talk.

449
00:25:32.280 --> 00:25:33.406
Certainly not the setting.

450
00:25:33.560 --> 00:25:35.562
While I was waiting,
a cockroach this big

451
00:25:35.640 --> 00:25:37.449
tried to steal my shoe.

452
00:25:37.640 --> 00:25:39.608
Yeah. They'll take a shoe.

453
00:25:41.320 --> 00:25:43.641
Honestly, this?

454
00:25:43.800 --> 00:25:47.646
This godforsaken mosquito
ranch was a conscious choice?

455
00:25:49.360 --> 00:25:51.761
I could drop you back off
at the airport.

456
00:25:57.520 --> 00:25:59.045
<b><font color="#808000">(CAR DOOR CLOSES)</font></b>

457
00:25:59.320 --> 00:26:00.970
<b><font color="#808000">(ENGINE STARTS)</font></b>

458
00:26:13.520 --> 00:26:16.842
This thing is loaded
with cool problems.

459
00:26:25.720 --> 00:26:27.563
<b><font color="#408080">EVELYN:</font></b> So, are you teaching?

460
00:26:27.720 --> 00:26:28.960
<b><font color="#408080">FRANK:</font></b> I repair boats.

461
00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:31.681
- Please.
- I'm not bad at it either.

462
00:26:31.840 --> 00:26:33.604
Well, then, that explains this.

463
00:26:33.760 --> 00:26:35.888
They don't sell sunscreen here?

464
00:26:36.040 --> 00:26:37.087
I wear sunscreen.

465
00:26:37.280 --> 00:26:39.487
<i>Not enough.
And you need a hat.</i>

466
00:26:39.640 --> 00:26:42.405
A big hat that shades
your face and neck.

467
00:26:42.560 --> 00:26:44.336
You're playing Russian
roulette with your skin.

468
00:26:44.360 --> 00:26:45.691
You look like a porn producer.

469
00:26:45.840 --> 00:26:48.366
Okay. I appreciate the advice.
I do.

470
00:26:48.520 --> 00:26:50.416
But we're not here to talk
about sunscreen, are we?

471
00:26:50.440 --> 00:26:53.205
Oh, so no more small talk?
That's a shame.

472
00:26:53.720 --> 00:26:56.929
Okay. The environment you have
created for that child

473
00:26:57.080 --> 00:26:58.809
where she lives,
the school she attends,

474
00:26:58.880 --> 00:27:00.200
it's substandard,
every bit of it.

475
00:27:00.280 --> 00:27:01.645
I disagree.

476
00:27:01.720 --> 00:27:02.767
We're getting nowhere

477
00:27:02.840 --> 00:27:03.976
if we're not gonna be honest
with each other.

478
00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:05.764
I am being honest.

479
00:27:05.920 --> 00:27:09.322
I see. Fine. Well, I'm not
leaving without her.

480
00:27:09.520 --> 00:27:12.126
Well, welcome to Florida.

481
00:27:12.280 --> 00:27:14.442
Frank, please listen to reason.

482
00:27:14.600 --> 00:27:16.921
At some point, either you're
gonna come to the conclusion,

483
00:27:17.000 --> 00:27:18.081
or someone in authority

484
00:27:18.160 --> 00:27:19.296
is going to spell it out for you

485
00:27:19.320 --> 00:27:21.607
that the child's best interest
is all that matters.

486
00:27:21.760 --> 00:27:24.127
You're gonna make me
pull rank, I will.

487
00:27:24.280 --> 00:27:25.691
Diane didn't want you
to have her.

488
00:27:26.440 --> 00:27:27.566
Diane...

489
00:27:29.360 --> 00:27:31.169
Diane didn't always
think things through.

490
00:27:31.320 --> 00:27:33.256
Arguably one of the brightest
minds on the planet.

491
00:27:33.280 --> 00:27:34.520
Good luck going down that road.

492
00:27:34.600 --> 00:27:35.816
And what do you think she'd say

493
00:27:35.840 --> 00:27:37.968
if she saw
how her child is living now?

494
