00:38:03.880 --> 00:38:05.450
Can you promise
that there's no way?

730
00:38:05.600 --> 00:38:07.284
Yes. Roberta has Mary

731
00:38:07.360 --> 00:38:08.536
every Saturday till noon,
I promise.

732
00:38:08.560 --> 00:38:10.483
- Yeah?
- Mmm-hmm.

733
00:38:13.120 --> 00:38:16.283
- Mmm. Mmm-mmm. Sorry.
- <b><font color="#808000">(SIGHS)</font></b>

734
00:38:16.480 --> 00:38:18.687
I can't do this.

735
00:38:20.680 --> 00:38:23.570
<b><font color="#808000">(EXHALES)</font></b> Okay.

736
00:38:24.640 --> 00:38:25.766
Okay.

737
00:38:27.120 --> 00:38:28.406
I understand.

738
00:38:29.840 --> 00:38:31.001
I'll take you home.

739
00:38:31.200 --> 00:38:32.929
Yeah.

740
00:38:33.120 --> 00:38:36.090
- Sorry, just... One more time.
- <b><font color="#808000">(LAUGHS)</font></b>

741
00:38:51.640 --> 00:38:53.051
<b><font color="#808000">(BLIND CLACKING)</font></b>

742
00:38:53.560 --> 00:38:55.801
<b><font color="#408080">MARY:</font></b> I can't find my DVD.

743
00:38:55.960 --> 00:38:57.610
<b><font color="#408080">ROBERTA:</font></b>
It's right there, baby.

744
00:38:57.760 --> 00:38:59.489
No, it isn't.
You come and find it.

745
00:38:59.640 --> 00:39:00.926
<b><font color="#408080">ROBERTA:</font></b>
I'm doing lady business.

746
00:39:01.080 --> 00:39:02.456
Now, you were the last person
to watch it.

747
00:39:02.480 --> 00:39:03.527
What did you do with it?

748
00:39:07.120 --> 00:39:08.360
Oh...

749
00:39:17.880 --> 00:39:19.370
<b><font color="#808000">(GROANING)</font></b>

750
00:39:27.400 --> 00:39:28.731
<b><font color="#808000">(SIGHS)</font></b>

751
00:39:37.160 --> 00:39:38.286
<b><font color="#808000">(GASPS)</font></b> Oh! Oh, my God!

752
00:39:44.160 --> 00:39:45.571
<b><font color="#808000">(EXHALES HEAVILY)</font></b>

753
00:39:48.440 --> 00:39:51.569
Good morning, Miss Stevenson.

754
00:39:52.800 --> 00:39:54.006
Hi.

755
00:39:55.800 --> 00:39:56.847
Look, I am...

756
00:39:56.920 --> 00:39:58.536
Oh, you know what?
I'm actually incapable...

757
00:39:58.560 --> 00:40:00.608
of having
this conversation right now.

758
00:40:00.760 --> 00:40:02.967
Good. 'Cause I have no idea
what I was gonna say.

759
00:40:03.280 --> 00:40:04.486
<b><font color="#808000">(CHUCKLES)</font></b>

760
00:40:04.560 --> 00:40:06.369
Great! Perfect!

761
00:40:06.520 --> 00:40:09.569
Sir, if you please.
Take me to jail.

762
00:40:18.080 --> 00:40:19.445
<b><font color="#808000">(SING-SONG)</font></b> Awkward.

763
00:40:20.800 --> 00:40:21.801
Mary...

764
00:40:21.960 --> 00:40:23.041
Stop! Stop!

765
00:40:23.200 --> 00:40:25.282
Stop with the Legos! Listen.

766
00:40:25.480 --> 00:40:27.130
Do we have a rule
about Saturday morning?

767
00:40:27.320 --> 00:40:28.526
What?

768
00:40:29.360 --> 00:40:30.496
Are you allowed
in this apartment this early

769
00:40:30.520 --> 00:40:31.720
on Saturday morning?
Yes or no?

770
00:40:31.840 --> 00:40:33.444
- No.
- No!

771
00:40:33.840 --> 00:40:34.887
<b><font color="#808000">(LEGOS RATTLING)</font></b>

772
00:40:34.960 --> 00:40:36.166
Are you allowed to... Hey!

773
00:40:36.320 --> 00:40:38.561
Stop! Enough with the Legos.

774
00:40:38.720 --> 00:40:40.643
Are you allowed to
use Roberta's keys?

775
00:40:40.800 --> 00:40:42.006
- No.
- No!

776
00:40:42.760 --> 00:40:45.161
Hey! Look at me.

777
00:40:45.320 --> 00:40:47.607
Then why are you here, huh?

778
00:40:48.200 --> 00:40:49.361
Can you answer me that?

779
00:40:49.600 --> 00:40:51.409
You broke every rule!

780
00:40:52.480 --> 00:40:54.608
You just embarrassed me.

781
00:40:54.800 --> 00:40:55.926
We have these rules.

782
00:40:56.080 --> 00:40:58.128
We've gone over them
a hundred times! Ahh!

783
00:40:59.240 --> 00:41:00.685
God!

784
00:41:00.840 --> 00:41:02.410
Damn it!

785
00:41:02.600 --> 00:41:04.682
Can I just get five minutes
of my own life?

786
00:41:10.920 --> 00:41:12.160
<b><font color="#808000">(SLAMS DOOR)</font></b>

787
00:41:28.360 --> 00:41:30.249
<b><font color="#808000">(FRANK SIGHS)</font></b>

788
00:41:34.120 --> 00:41:36.282
Nothing that happened today
was your fault.

789
00:41:36.440 --> 00:41:38.044
I got mad at you...

790
00:41:38.200 --> 00:41:40.009
I was really mad at me.

791
00:41:40.920 --> 00:41:42.684
<i>And the manufacturers oi Legos,</i>

792
00:41:42.760 --> 00:41:44.125
they should all be in prison.

793
00:41:48.680 --> 00:41:50.125
I'm sorry.

794
00:41:52.080 --> 00:41:53.445
Do you forgive me?

795
00:41:54.640 --> 00:41:56.130
Sure. Whatever.

796
00:41:56.760 --> 00:41:59.127
Hey, close the laptop.
Come on.

797
00:41:59.720 --> 00:42:02.371
Please. Doesn't count
if it's not eye to eye.

798
00:42:03.520 --> 00:42:05.045
Come on, please.

799
00:42:06.440 --> 00:42:07.771
<b><font color="#808000">(SIGHS)</font></b>

800
00:42:08.200 --> 00:42:10.441
Do you really have no life
because of me?

801
00:42:12.680 --> 00:42:14.250
That's not what I said.

802
00:42:14.480 --> 00:42:15.891
Hmm.

803
00:42:16.960 --> 00:42:18.485
Did you mean it?

804
00:42:21.160 --> 00:42:22.207
Last month you said I was

805
00:42:22.280 --> 00:42:23.376
the worst uncle in the world...

806
00:42:23.400 --> 00:42:24.686
and you wished death upon me

807
00:42:24.760 --> 00:42:26.040
'cause I didn't buy you a piano.

808
00:42:26.520 --> 00:42:28.010
Did you mean that?

809
00:42:29.160 --> 00:42:30.605
No.

810
00:42:30.760 --> 00:42:32.489
Not entirely.

811
00:42:35.240 --> 00:42:36.287
There you go.

812
00:42:36.440 --> 00:42:38.920
We say things all the time
we don't mean. So...

813
00:42:40.680 --> 00:42:42.205
Let's forget it, okay?

814
00:42:44.280 --> 00:42:45.281
Okay.

815
00:42:45.920 --> 00:42:47.365
Okay.

816
00:42:49.040 --> 00:42:50.610
<b><font color="#808000">(FRANK SIGHS)</font></b>

817
00:42:50.960 --> 00:42:52.041
Frank?

818
00:42:52.200 --> 00:42:53.201
Yeah?

819
00:42:53.880 --> 00:42:54.881
Can I have a piano?

820
00:42:55.040 --> 00:42:56.087
No.

821
00:42:58.000 --> 00:42:59.729
Was that really Mary's teacher
this morning?

822
00:43:00.200 --> 00:43:01.361
You know, I got a book called

823
00:43:01.440 --> 00:43:03.090
<i>Fundamentals of Decision Making.</i>

824
00:43:03.320 --> 00:43:04.401
You can borrow it.

825
00:43:13.240 --> 00:43:14.571
What is it?

826
00:43:15.600 --> 00:43:17.364
It's nothing.

827
00:43:17.520 --> 00:43:20.046
Looks like Mary gets to go
to Boston for a couple days.

828
00:43:23.880 --> 00:43:25.564
It's just two days.

829
00:43:26.440 --> 00:43:27.441
Relax.

830
00:43:37.600 --> 00:43:39.170
<b><font color="#408080">EVELYN:</font></b> So, what do you think?

831
00:43:40.040 --> 00:43:41.405
<b><font color="#408080">MARY:</font></b> Cool.

832
00:43:54.200 --> 00:43:56.362
Wow! You and my mom...

833
00:43:56.520 --> 00:43:58.010
were always together.

834
00:43:59.320 --> 00:44:00.367
Always.

835
00:44:21.840 --> 00:44:23.729
Oh, no, you don't want
to look at that one.

836
00:44:24.000 --> 00:44:25.001
Is that you?

837
00:44:28.600 --> 00:44:29.601
Yes.

838
00:44:30.360 --> 00:44:31.805
<b><font color="#808000">(BOTH CHUCKLE)</font></b>

839
00:44:32.160 --> 00:44:33.366
How old were you?

840
00:44:33.880 --> 00:44:34.881
<b><font color="#408080">EVELYN:</font></b> 29.

841
00:44:36.400 --> 00:44:38.323
<b><font color="#408080">MARY:</font></b> Where is this?

842
00:44:38.480 --> 00:44:40.562
That's Cambridge University.

843
00:44:40.720 --> 00:44:42.722
In England.

844
00:44:47.560 --> 00:44:48.766
<b><font color="#408080">MARY:</font></b> Who are those people?

845
00:44:48.920 --> 00:44:50.729
My research colleagues.

846
00:44:51.560 --> 00:44:52.721
For what?

847
00:44:52.880 --> 00:44:54.689
Mathematics, of course.

848
00:44:55.440 --> 00:44:57.044
Really?

849
00:44:58.920 --> 00:45:00.524
Cool!

850
00:45:03.080 --> 00:45:04.844
So, then you came to work...

851
00:45:05.000 --> 00:45:06.161
at a college in America?

852
00:45:06.720 --> 00:45:07.767
<b><font color="#408080">EVELYN:</font></b> No.

853
00:45:07.920 --> 00:45:10.651
I married your grandfather...

854
00:45:10.800 --> 00:45:13.167
and I came back
to America with him.

855
00:45:14.200 --> 00:45:16.202
And I had children.

856
00:45:16.440 --> 00:45:17.646
Oh.

857
00:45:18.760 --> 00:45:19.921
So, no more math?

858
00:45:22.880 --> 00:45:23.927
It's late.

859
00:45:24.120 --> 00:45:25.849
You've got a big day tomorrow.

860
00:45:26.000 --> 00:45:28.970
Come on. Up, up, up.

861
00:45:29.160 --> 00:45:30.491
<b><font color="#408080">MARY:</font></b> Can I play the piano?

862
00:45:30.560 --> 00:45:31.561
<b><font color="#408080">EVELYN:</font></b> No, you can't.

863
00:45:31.640 --> 00:45:33.296
- Now stop dragging those feet.
- But, it's fun!

864
00:45:33.320 --> 00:45:34.320
<b><font color="#408080">EVELYN:</font></b> Come on!

865
00:45:35.080 --> 00:45:38.562
<b><font color="#408080">BONNIE:</font></b> I have had
a series of nightmares...

866
00:45:38.720 --> 00:45:41.007
where I'm fired...

867
00:45:42.120 --> 00:45:44.566
because of what happened.

868
00:45:44.800 --> 00:45:46.211
<b><font color="#808000">(CHUCKLES)</font></b> You get it?

869
00:45:46.800 --> 00:45:48.290
And then I try to rationalize...

870
00:45:48.440 --> 00:45:52.126
that everything that happened
was just all alcohol.

871
00:45:52.200 --> 00:45:53.361
<b><font color="#808000">(CHUCKLES)</font></b>

872
00:45:57.160 --> 00:45:59.003
I have an addiction
to fixer guys.

873
00:45:59.960 --> 00:46:01.246
<b><font color="#808000">(FRANK LAUGHS)</font></b>

874
00:46:14.040 --> 00:46:15.963
Dr. Shankland,
this problem is...

875
00:46:16.120 --> 00:46:19.329
I know. Just copy it exactly
as you see it written.

876
00:46:19.640 --> 00:46:20.687
But it's...

877
00:46:20.920 --> 00:46:23.491
Exactly as written. Thank you.

878
00:46:28.840 --> 00:46:31.446
<b><font color="#408080">MARY:</font></b> <i>So, what's this problem
I'm supposed to look at?</i>

879
00:46:31.520 --> 00:46:33.329
I don't know.

880
00:46:33.480 --> 00:46:35.767
Is it like the problems
Mom worked on?

881
00:46:35.920 --> 00:46:38.082
Your mother didn't work
on problems.

882
00:46:38.240 --> 00:46:40.402
She worked on just one problem.

883
00:46:40.560 --> 00:46:42.324
Just one?

884
00:46:42.480 --> 00:46:44.050
Her entire life?

885
00:46:44.200 --> 00:46:45.929
Most of it.

886
00:46:46.080 --> 00:46:47.286
Look.

887
00:46:48.960 --> 00:46:52.043
These are
the Millennium Problems.

888
00:46:52.840 --> 00:46:56.242
Seven great
and meaningful problems.

889
00:46:56.400 --> 00:46:58.482
Some mathematicians have worked

890
00:46:58.560 --> 00:47:00.449
their entire lives
to prove them.

891
00:47:01.200 --> 00:47:02.770
Who's the dude with the beard?

892
00:47:02.960 --> 00:47:04.371
<b><font color="#808000">(CHUCKLES)</font></b> That's not a dude.

893
00:47:04.520 --> 00:47:06.761
That's Grigori Perelman.

894
00:47:06.920 --> 00:47:09.446
He proved
the Poincare conjecture.

895
00:47:09.600 --> 00:47:12.683
The only one
of the seven proved.

896
00:47:12.840 --> 00:47:14.444
This...

897
00:47:16.320 --> 00:47:18.527
This is your mother's problem.

898
00:47:19.760 --> 00:47:20.841
Na... vi...

899
00:47:21.000 --> 00:47:23.731
Navier-Stokes.

900
00:47:25.440 --> 00:47:27.249
No picture.

901
00:47:27.400 --> 00:47:29.243
- She didn't solve it?
- No.

902
00:47:30.320 --> 00:47:31.731
She was close.

903
00:47:33.120 --> 00:47:35.009
She would have won
the Fields Medal...

904
00:47:35.680 --> 00:47:37.569
and probably shared a Nobel...

905
00:47:37.720 --> 00:47:40.087
considering what it would have
meant for physics.

906
00:47:40.240 --> 00:47:43.130
Maybe I'll have my picture
up here someday.

907
00:47:49.240 --> 00:47:51.004
If you really desire it...

908
00:47:51.160 --> 00:47:53.891
you can have
your picture there, darling.

909
00:47:54.040 --> 00:47:56.327
I can help you.

910
00:47:56.480 --> 00:48:00.246
It takes focus and hard work,
but if you succeed...

911
00:48:01.320 --> 00:48:04.130
your name will live forever.

912
00:48:14.400 --> 00:48:16.528
Don't be smug, Seymore.

913
00:48:16.920 --> 00:48:19.002
Well, she's had plenty of time.

914
00:48:19.160 --> 00:48:22.687
She traveled yesterday.
She slept in a strange bed.

915
00:48:23.280 --> 00:48:25.362
Give her a chance.

916
00:48:27.880 --> 00:48:30.201
At six years old,
she read Zimmer.

917
00:48:31.440 --> 00:48:32.601
Outstanding.

918
00:48:33.440 --> 00:48:35.681
How much did she comprehend?

919
00:48:37.600 --> 00:48:38.886
So, Mary...

920
00:48:39.920 --> 00:48:42.730
I see you're looking
at our little problem.

921
00:48:43.040 --> 00:48:45.247
<b><font color="#408080">MARY:</font></b> Little? It's big.

922
00:48:46.000 --> 00:48:47.001
Yeah.

923
00:48:53.240 --> 00:48:54.969
<b><font color="#408080">MARY:</font></b> Why are you so mad
all of a sudden?

924
00:48:55.160 --> 00:48:57.288
I'm not mad. I'm annoyed.

925
00:48:57.440 --> 00:48:58.487
Not with you, dear.

926
00:48:58.560 --> 00:49:00.847
With that pompous ass,
Shankland.

927
00:49:01.040 --> 00:49:02.769
I knew that guy was gonna
have a beard

928
00:49:02.840 --> 00:49:04.490
before we even went in there.

929
00:49:04.640 --> 00:49:06.642
Math teachers
like to grow beards.

930
00:49:06.800 --> 00:49:09.167
I should never have agreed
to this in the first place.

931
00:49:09.320 --> 00:49:10.685
Did he really expect you

932
00:49:10.800 --> 00:49:12.216
to just walk in
and be able to dissect...

933
00:49:12.240 --> 00:49:14.288
some random, massive problem?

934
00:49:14.440 --> 00:49:17.489
Not much to dissect,
if you ask me.

935
00:49:17.680 --> 00:49:19.045
Why do you say that?

936
00:49:19.120 --> 00:49:20.121
It was wrong.

937
00:49:21.120 --> 00:49:22.201
What?

938
00:49:22.360 --> 00:49:24.522
Well, for starters,

939
00:49:24.600 --> 00:49:26.762
he forgot the negative sign
on the exponent.

940
00:49:26.920 --> 00:49:29.002
It went downhill from there.

941
00:49:29.160 --> 00:49:30.730
The problem was unsolvable.

942
00:49:31.920 --> 00:49:35.208
Maybe this school isn't
as great as you think it is.

943
00:50:48.280 --> 00:50:49.930
Mary, you knew
the problem was incorrect.

944
00:50:50.000 --> 00:50:51.161
Why didn't you say anything?

945
00:50:51.360 --> 00:50:54.204
Frank says I'm not supposed to
correct older people.

946
00:50:54.440 --> 00:50:55.885
<b><font color="#808000">(CHUCKLES)</font></b>

947
00:50:56.120 --> 00:50:58.964
Nobody likes a smart-ass.

948
00:51:05.880 --> 00:51:08.042
I'm loaded with swag!

949
00:51:16.320 --> 00:51:17.560
Wanna come inside?

950
00:51:19.520 --> 00:51:20.521
Cat.

951
00:51:21.520 --> 00:51:23.682
<b><font color="#408080">MARY:</font></b> Frank,
I think this is stupid.

952
00:51:23.840 --> 00:51:25.888
Why don't we just call Evelyn

953
00:51:25.960 --> 00:51:27.320
and tell her
I don't wanna do this?

954
00:51:27.360 --> 00:51:28.600
Because like I told you,

955
00:51:28.680 --> 00:51:30.240
Evelyn didn't order this.
The court did.

956
00:51:30.360 --> 00:51:31.805
So again, what are we gonna do?

957
00:51:31.920 --> 00:51:32.967
Tell the truth.

958
00:51:33.040 --> 00:51:34.041
Exactly.

959
00:51:34.200 --> 00:51:35.456
Sooner we answer
these questions,

960
00:51:35.480 --> 00:51:36.561
sooner we get to go home.

961
00:51:36.640 --> 00:51:37.926
So, no attitude, okay?

962
00:51:38.080 --> 00:51:39.684
Okay.

963
00:51:41.560 --> 00:51:43.324
<b><font color="#808000">(SIGHS)</font></b> Frank?

964
00:51:43.480 --> 00:51:44.527
Mary.

965
00:51:45.840 --> 00:51:47.410
I like Evelyn.

966
00:51:47.560 --> 00:51:49.164
She looks like my mom.

967
00:51:49.320 --> 00:51:50.810
And she has a piano.

968
00:51:55.320 --> 00:51:57.641
But I don't wanna live with her.

969
00:51:57.840 --> 00:51:59.001
She's bossy.

970
00:52:00.320 --> 00:52:02.368
I've heard that.

971
00:52:02.720 --> 00:52:04.051
Hey.

972
00:52:05.080 --> 00:52:07.731
Don't worry.
You're not going anywhere.

973
00:52:09.840 --> 00:52:10.841
Promise?

974
00:52:13.960 --> 00:52:15.291
I promise.

975
00:52:15.480 --> 00:52:17.528
Hi, Mary. I'm Pat Golding,

976
00:52:17.600 --> 00:52:19.000
but you can call me Pat
if you like.

977
00:52:19.080 --> 00:52:20.730
Got it.

978
00:52:20.880 --> 00:52:22.644
Do you know why you're here?

979
00:52:22.800 --> 00:52:25.121
I mean has anyone told you
what this is about?

980
00:52:25.280 --> 00:52:26.840
My grandmother wants me
to live with her

981
00:52:26.880 --> 00:52:28.166
and I wanna stay with Frank.

982
00:52:28.320 --> 00:52:30.243
That's correct.

983
00:52:30.400 --> 00:52:32.607
I'm sure this can all be
very scary stuff.

984
00:52:32.680 --> 00:52:34.523
Does any of it worry you?

985
00:52:34.680 --> 00:52:37.650
Nope. Frank says
I'm not goin' anywhere.

986
00:52:38.560 --> 00:52:40.360
Your uncle told you
you're not going anywhere?

987
00:52:40.480 --> 00:52:42.244
Just 15 minutes ago.

988
00:52:42.320 --> 00:52:43.367
So, we done here?

989
00:52:44.960 --> 00:52:46.371
Not quite yet.

990
00:52:46.880 --> 00:52:49.281
My best friend other than
Frank and Fred is Roberta...

991
00:52:49.440 --> 00:52:50.805
who lives a couple houses away.

992
00:52:50.960 --> 00:52:52.086
Really? What's she like?

993
00:52:52.240 --> 00:52:55.403
She's nice and funny.
And I love her.

994
00:52:55.480 --> 00:52:57.960
<b><font color="#808000">(CHUCKLES)</font></b> That's nice.

995
00:52:58.120 --> 00:53:00.168
Is Roberta your only friend
your age?

996
00:53:00.480 --> 00:53:03.927
Roberta's not my age.
She's like 40, 50...

997
00:53:04.080 --> 00:53:05.730
30-something.

998
00:53:05.880 --> 00:53:07.689
Roberta's a grown woman?

999
00:53:07.840 --> 00:53:10.047
People my age are boring.

1000
00:53:10.200 --> 00:53:11.884
Roberta's cool.

1001
00:53:12.760 --> 00:53:13.886
Okay.

1002
00:53:14.080 --> 00:53:15.206
I don't watch TV.

1003
00:53:15.360 --> 00:53:17.522
But I got hooked on SpongeBob.

1004
00:53:17.680 --> 00:53:19.648
Mostly because Roberta loves it.

1005
00:53:21.560 --> 00:53:24.609
Oh. And on Saturday nights,
we watch the UFC.

1006
00:53:24.800 --> 00:53:26.768
The UFC? ls that...

1007
00:53:26.920 --> 00:53:28.081
<i>ls</i> that <i>Ultimate Fighting?</i>

1008
00:53:28.240 --> 00:53:30.208
Every Saturday.

1009
00:53:30.360 --> 00:53:32.647
<i>You and your</i> uncle <i>watch
Ultimate Fighting?</i>

1010
00:53:32.800 --> 00:53:35.087
Well, you don't just watch it.

1011
00:53:35.440 --> 00:53:36.851
Frank turns the sound down

1012
00:53:36.920 --> 00:53:38.649
and we do the grunting
and voices.

1013
00:53:39.480 --> 00:53:40.527
Hmm.

1014
00:53:40.600 --> 00:53:42.523
Sometimes I attack Frank
for real.

1015
00:53:42.680 --> 00:53:44.682
Which is really fun for me.

1016
00:53:44.840 --> 00:53:46.808
I think if you guys knew Frank,

1017
00:53:46.880 --> 00:53:48.370
you'd leave us alone.

1018
00:53:49.000 --> 00:53:50.923
He can be pretty annoying...

1019
00:53:51.120 --> 00:53:53.771
but he's a good person.

1020
00:53:53.920 --> 00:53:55.081
I think.

1021
00:53:56.280 --> 00:53:57.611
Why do you say that?

1022
00:53:59.120 --> 00:54:01.441
He wanted me before I was smart.

1023
00:54:01.880 --> 00:54:03.245
<b><font color="#408080">HIGHSMITH:</font></b>
Mr. Pollard, are you...

1024
00:54:03.400 --> 00:54:06.131
the natural father
of Mary Adler?

1025
00:54:06.280 --> 00:54:07.770
Yes, I am.

1026
00:54:07.920 --> 00:54:09.490
And how can you be
certain of this?

1027
00:54:09.960 --> 00:54:13.089
<b><font color="#408080">POLLARD:</font></b> Well, I always knew,
but then you had me...

1028
00:54:13.240 --> 00:54:14.730
take a DNA test.

1029
00:54:14.880 --> 00:54:16.484
I would offer the test results

1030
00:54:16.560 --> 00:54:18.688
as presumptive evidence
that Mr. Pollard...

1031
00:54:18.840 --> 00:54:21.889
is the father and natural
guardian of the minor.

1032
00:54:22.040 --> 00:54:23.530
As well as an affidavit...

1033
00:54:23.680 --> 00:54:25.523
from Mr. Pollard nominating...

1034
00:54:25.680 --> 00:54:27.682
Mrs. Adler,
the maternal grandmother,

1035
00:54:27.760 --> 00:54:29.603
as the legal guardian
of the minor.

1036
00:54:30.400 --> 00:54:31.447
No objections.

1037
00:54:33.600 --> 00:54:35.284
Mr. Pollard...

1038
00:54:35.440 --> 00:54:38.922
has Mrs. Adler offered you
any monetary reward...

1039
00:54:39.080 --> 00:54:41.560
or employment
for coming forward today?

1040
00:54:42.280 --> 00:54:44.567
No, sir.
I have a job of my own.

1041
00:54:44.760 --> 00:54:45.761
<b><font color="#408080">HIGHSMITH:</font></b> Thank you.

1042
00:54:45.840 --> 00:54:47.524
No further questions,
Your Honor.

1043
00:54:47.720 --> 00:54:48.846
<b><font color="#408080">CULLEN:</font></b> Mr. Pollard...

1044
00:54:49.000 --> 00:54:51.367
when was the last time
you saw Mary?

1045
00:54:52.640 --> 00:54:55.120
Uh... I've never seen her.

1046
00:54:55.200 --> 00:54:56.486
<b><font color="#408080">CULLEN:</font></b> Oh, why not?

1047
00:54:56.600 --> 00:54:58.443
<b><font color="#408080">POLLARD:</font></b> By the time
I heard about Diane...

1048
00:54:59.320 --> 00:55:01.084
Passing...

1049
00:55:01.240 --> 00:55:03.049
the baby was gone already.

1050
00:55:03.200 --> 00:55:05.407
<b><font color="#408080">CULLEN:</font></b> Well, did you try
and find her?

1051
00:55:06.480 --> 00:55:08.084
Best I could.

1052
00:55:08.880 --> 00:55:11.201
I couldn't just go
and search the entire country.

1053
00:55:11.360 --> 00:55:13.408
<b><font color="#408080">CULLEN:</font></b> Do you use
a computer at work?

1054
00:55:13.480 --> 00:55:14.481
<b><font color="#408080">POLLARD:</font></b> Sure.

1055
00:55:14.560 --> 00:55:16.403
You know what? Help me out.

1056
00:55:17.960 --> 00:55:19.928
Here, let's google "Mary Adler"

1057
00:55:20.000 --> 00:55:22.367
and, uh, see what we find.

1058
00:55:30.200 --> 00:55:31.964
<b><font color="#808000">(EXHALES DEEPLY)</font></b>

1059
00:55:32.720 --> 00:55:34.131
You know what?

1060
00:55:34.280 --> 00:55:35.884
Uh, you better add
her middle name.

1061
00:55:36.400 --> 00:55:38.164
It'll narrow it down.

1062
00:55:42.800 --> 00:55:44.086
Eileen.

1063
00:55:52.000 --> 00:55:53.968
Yeah. Hit Enter.

1064
00:55:54.560 --> 00:55:55.680
- <b><font color="#808000">(KEYBOARD CLACKS)</font></b>
- Page 2.

1065
00:55:56.120 --> 00:55:57.246
Second hit.

1066
00:55:58.000 --> 00:56:00.606
Would you please tell
the court what you see there?

1067
00:56:00.760 --> 00:56:02.762
It's a newspaper article called

1068
00:56:02.880 --> 00:56:04.041
"Not So Terrible Twos."

1069
00:56:04.200 --> 00:56:07.761
And one of them
is Mary Eileen Adler.

1070
00:56:07.920 --> 00:56:09.604
Same name as your daughter.

1071
00:56:09.800 --> 00:56:12.451
Born on the same day
as your daughter.

1072
00:56:12.600 --> 00:56:14.602
With a photograph.

1073
00:56:14.760 --> 00:56:16.416
Um, in your defense,
you'd never recognize it...

1074
00:56:16.440 --> 00:56:18.136
- Your Honor, this is...
- <b><font color="#408080">CULLEN:</font></b> Your Honor...

1075
00:56:18.160 --> 00:56:20.322
if there's one thing here
that's sadly obvious...

1076
00:56:20.520 --> 00:56:23.205
it's that Mr. Pollard
has never been

1077
00:56:23.280 --> 00:56:24.930
a genuine guardian
of the minor...

1078
00:56:25.080 --> 00:56:26.969
and his nomination
of Mrs. Adler

1079
00:56:27.040 --> 00:56:28.610
is no less disingenuous.

1080
00:56:33.920 --> 00:56:35.560
While the State of Florida
gives deference

1081
00:56:35.600 --> 00:56:37.011
to nomination
by a natural parent...

1082
00:56:37.200 --> 00:56:38.936
I'm inclined to side
with Mr. Cullen's laptop...

1083
00:56:38.960 --> 00:56:40.200
at this time.

1084
00:56:40.360 --> 00:56:41.486
Thank you, Your Honor.

1085
00:56:49.360 --> 00:56:52.170
<b><font color="#808000">(PEOPLE MURMURING)</font></b>

1086
00:56:53.960 --> 00:56:55.041
Don't gloat.

1087
00:56:55.200 --> 00:56:56.884
Wouldn't dream of it.

1088
00:56:57.040 --> 00:56:58.087
Walk you to your car?

1089
00:56:58.240 --> 00:57:00.971
We've been so preoccupied,
I'm almost ashamed to ask.

1090
00:57:01.120 --> 00:57:03.009
- How's Walter?
- <b><font color="#808000">(SCOFFS)</font></b>

1091
00:57:04.120 --> 00:57:06.122
You wouldn't believe it
if I told you.

1092
00:57:06.200 --> 00:57:07.201
Why?

1093
00:57:07.280 --> 00:57:11.001
Well, your stepfather
is in Montana.

1094
00:57:11.160 --> 00:57:12.969
He bought a ranch.

1095
00:57:13.040 --> 00:57:14.041
Bullshit.

1096
00:57:14.120 --> 00:57:16.930
Exactly. A man whose idea
of roughing it...

1097
00:57:17.120 --> 00:57:19.964
is being too far from the ice
machine at the Ritz-Carlton...

1098
00:57:20.160 --> 00:57:23.323
now owns a 1,000 acres
of grass and dung.

1099
00:57:23.480 --> 00:57:26.086
Walter Price is a cowboy.

1100
00:57:26.240 --> 00:57:28.288
Walter Price puts on
a Brooks Brothers suit

1101
00:57:28.360 --> 00:57:29.566
to take out the garbage.

1102
00:57:29.720 --> 00:57:32.929
He has a cowboy hat
and cowboy boots...

1103
00:57:33.080 --> 00:57:35.367
and a horse
that doesn't know dressage.

1104
00:57:35.520 --> 00:57:37.284
Is there some
logical reason for this?

1105
00:57:37.440 --> 00:57:40.046
Midlife crisis, apparently.

1106
00:57:40.640 --> 00:57:41.641
He's 70.

1107
00:57:41.800 --> 00:57:42.961
I know.

1108
00:57:43.680 --> 00:57:46.160
Must have been on time delay
or something.

1109
00:57:46.960 --> 00:57:48.041
I guess I should be happy

1110
00:57:48.120 --> 00:57:50.521
it wasn't a 25-year-old
cocktail waitress.

1111
00:57:51.440 --> 00:57:52.680
But then again,

1112
00:57:52.760 --> 00:57:55.001
an affair you can explain
to friends in a minute.

1113
00:57:55.200 --> 00:57:58.602
For this, you put on
a pot of coffee.

1114
00:58:00.200 --> 00:58:01.645
And he's out there right now?

1115
00:58:01.800 --> 00:58:02.847
Yes siree!

1116
00:58:02.920 --> 00:58:06.208
Ridin' the range.
Punchin' doggies.

1117
00:58:06.360 --> 00:58:08.601
<b><font color="#808000">(IMITATES JOHN WAYNE)</font></b>
He had a six gun on his hip...

1118
00:58:08.760 --> 00:58:11.684
and a saddlebag full of Lipitor.

1119
00:58:13.040 --> 00:58:16.203
The fastest asset management
in the West.

1120
00:58:16.360 --> 00:58:18.806
The man who shot Liberty Mutual.

1121
00:58:19.880 --> 00:58:21.882
That's what I've been
calling him.

1122
00:58:24.600 --> 00:58:27.570
- <b><font color="#808000">(CHUCKLES)</font></b>
- That's really, really funny.

1123
00:58:36.040 --> 00:58:38.008
Take her to the airport.

1124
00:58:40.880 --> 00:58:42.609
Go home, Evelyn.

1125
00:58:42.760 --> 00:58:45.081
Or Montana.
Rustle some cattle.

1126
00:58:47.200 --> 00:58:48.201
You know...

1127
00:58:49.280 --> 00:58:52.329
I have no desire to hurt you.

1128
00:58:52.480 --> 00:58:54.448
I hate it that we're at odds.

1129
00:58:56.400 --> 00:58:57.925
We're always at odds.

1130
00:58:59.120 --> 00:59:00.246
Yes.

1131
00:59:01.080 --> 00:59:02.491
Hotel.

1132
00:59:06.400 --> 00:59:08.243
<b><font color="#408080">ROBERTA:</font></b> Mary, baby,
come on HOW.

1133
00:59:08.320 --> 00:59:09.321
Stop this now.

1134
00:59:09.680 --> 00:59:10.806
<b><font color="#808000">(CRYING)</font></b> No.

1135
00:59:10.960 --> 00:59:13.406
- Come on out, honey.
- No.

1136
00:59:13.480 --> 00:59:15.376
Why did you have to tell her
that deadbeat was testifying?

1137
00:59:15.400 --> 00:59:16.481
Because it's the truth.

1138
00:59:16.560 --> 00:59:17.840
And if I didn't,
Evelyn would've.

1139
00:59:17.960 --> 00:59:19.962
If I was the dad
of a little girl

1140
00:59:20.040 --> 00:59:21.565
and I never saw her...

1141
00:59:22.720 --> 00:59:24.961
and I was in the same town...

1142
00:59:26.120 --> 00:59:27.451
I would visit her.

1143
00:59:27.600 --> 00:59:29.090
Well, I hope you're happy.

1144
00:59:32.920 --> 00:59:35.810
He didn't even need directions.

1145
00:59:35.960 --> 00:59:38.725
He could've followed you here.

1146
00:59:39.480 --> 00:59:41.562
Speaking of men
who aren't worth a damn.

1147
00:59:43.800 --> 00:59:45.689
<b><font color="#808000">(CRYING)</font></b>

1148
00:59:48.040 --> 00:59:51.249
Doesn't even wanna see
what I look like.

1149
00:59:55.400 --> 00:59:56.481
All right.

1150
00:59:56.560 --> 00:59:58.449
Hey, put your shoes on.
We're going for a ride.

1151
00:59:58.560 --> 00:59:59.561
You too.

1152
01:00:00.280 --> 01:00:01.645
A ride?

1153
01:00:02.480 --> 01:00:04.687
<b><font color="#408080">MARY:</font></b> What are we doing here?

1154
01:00:04.840 --> 01:00:06.524
- <b><font color="#408080">FRANK:</font></b> Waiting.
- <b><font color="#808000">(ANNOUNCEMENT ON PA)</font></b>

1155
01:00:06.680 --> 01:00:07.761
We can see that.

1156
01:00:08.200 --> 01:00:09.201
Why?

1157
01:00:09.800 --> 01:00:11.564
Because I said so.

1158
01:00:13.640 --> 01:00:16.723
How long do we have to
stay here?

1159
01:00:16.920 --> 01:00:18.684
As long as it takes.

1160
01:00:18.880 --> 01:00:21.963
And keep your voice down.
It's a hospital.

1161
01:00:25.040 --> 01:00:27.247
- Food's here.
- Yeah, food's here.

1162
01:00:33.320 --> 01:00:35.448
<b><font color="#808000">(INDISTINCT CONVERSATION)</font></b>

1163
01:01:45.360 --> 01:01:47.362
<b><font color="#808000">(EXHALES)</font></b> It's a boy.

1164
01:01:47.440 --> 01:01:49.966
<b><font color="#808000">(ALL CHEERING EXCITEDLY)</font></b>

1165
01:01:54.360 --> 01:01:55.361
Way to go!

1166
01:02:00.920 --> 01:02:02.604
<b><font color="#808000">(LAUGHING)</font></b>

1167
01:02:04.120 --> 01:02:07.169
That's exactly how it was
when you were born.

1168
01:02:08.720 --> 01:02:10.085
This happy?

1169
01:02:10.440 --> 01:02:11.771
This happy-

1170
01:02:19.720 --> 01:02:22.166
Who came out and told everybody?

1171
01:02:24.760 --> 01:02:26.091
I did.

1172
01:02:35.000 --> 01:02:37.048
Can we stay for another?

1173
01:02:43.720 --> 01:02:46.291
<b><font color="#808000">(INAUDIBLE)</font></b>

1174
01:03:27.720 --> 01:03:30.291
Mrs. Adler, in your earlier
testimony, here today...

1175
01:03:30.440 --> 01:03:32.602
you've painted a pretty dim
picture of your own son.

1176
01:03:32.680 --> 01:03:33.806
Don't you think?

1177
01:03:33.880 --> 01:03:35.530
I'm under oath.

1178
01:03:35.680 --> 01:03:37.444
I take no pleasure in it.

1179
01:03:38.200 --> 01:03:39.850
So, your son's
a failure in life,

1180
01:03:39.960 --> 01:03:41.080
your daughter took her life.

1181
01:03:41.120 --> 01:03:42.240
You know, you're oh for two.

1182
01:03:42.320 --> 01:03:43.845
- <b><font color="#408080">HIGHSMITH:</font></b> Objection!
- Withdrawn.

1183
01:03:44.000 --> 01:03:46.651
If I go one for three,
I'm in the Hall of Fame.

1184
01:03:47.840 --> 01:03:49.763
You know baseball.

1185
01:03:49.920 --> 01:03:50.921
Fenway Park.

1186
01:03:51.000 --> 01:03:52.600
You know, I'd like to go there
some time.

1187
01:03:53.000 --> 01:03:55.128
How often in a year

1188
01:03:55.200 --> 01:03:56.850
did you take Diane
to a baseball game?

1189
01:03:57.000 --> 01:03:58.650
Diane wasn't interested
in sports.

1190
01:03:58.800 --> 01:04:00.928
She never wanted to go
to a game? Ever?

1191
01:04:01.080 --> 01:04:03.651
I don't recall her ever asking.

1192
01:04:03.800 --> 01:04:05.882
Just out of curiosity, Fenway,
that's a tough ticket.

1193
01:04:06.040 --> 01:04:07.087
Where do you get yours?

1194
01:04:07.280 --> 01:04:08.616
<b><font color="#408080">EVELYN:</font></b> My husband has
season tickets.

1195
01:04:08.640 --> 01:04:09.880
And how long has he had them?

1196
01:04:10.440 --> 01:04:11.487
30 years.

1197
01:04:11.560 --> 01:04:13.688
But I've only been
married to him for 20.

1198
01:04:13.840 --> 01:04:15.922
And Diane never went
to one game?

1199
01:04:17.680 --> 01:04:19.887
What color was the dress
Diane wore to prom?

1200
01:04:20.080 --> 01:04:21.650
Diane didn't attend a prom

1201
01:04:21.720 --> 01:04:22.776
because she didn't go
to a high school.

1202
01:04:22.800 --> 01:04:23.801
No prom.

1203
01:04:23.920 --> 01:04:25.001
What sports did she play?

1204
01:04:25.160 --> 01:04:26.321
As I told you earlier,

1205
01:04:26.400 --> 01:04:28.084
she wasn't interested in sports.

1206
01:04:28.160 --> 01:04:29.376
Did she go to camp
during the summer?

1207
01:04:29.400 --> 01:04:30.447
<b><font color="#408080">EVELYN:</font></b> No.

1208
01:04:30.520 --> 01:04:32.682
- Community swimming pool?
- We had our own pool.

1209
01:04:32.800 --> 01:04:34.131
- Girl Scouts?
- No.

1210
01:04:34.280 --> 01:04:37.841
Then what did she do
with all that time?

1211
01:04:38.000 --> 01:04:40.480
She loved mathematics.
It was her passion.

1212
01:04:40.640 --> 01:04:43.086
She preferred it
to all other things.

1213
01:04:43.240 --> 01:04:44.480
All other?

1214
01:04:46.360 --> 01:04:49.603
Mrs. Adler, who's Paul Riva?

1215
01:04:53.240 --> 01:04:54.730
<b><font color="#808000">(SCOFFS)</font></b>

1216
01:04:55.440 --> 01:04:56.930
He was a boy
from the neighborhood.

1217
01:04:57.120 --> 01:04:59.487
<b><font color="#408080">CULLEN:</font></b> Oh, come on.
He was much more than...

1218
01:04:59.640 --> 01:05:01.290
just a boy
from the neighborhood.

1219
01:05:01.440 --> 01:05:03.010
Paul was Diane's first love...

1220
01:05:03.080 --> 01:05:04.206
wasn't he?

1221
01:05:04.280 --> 01:05:06.362
I wouldn't characterize it
that way. No.

1222
01:05:06.520 --> 01:05:08.363
<b><font color="#408080">CULLEN:</font></b> And how would Diane
characterize it?

1223
01:05:08.520 --> 01:05:10.568
Diane was 17 years old
at the time.

1224
01:05:10.760 --> 01:05:12.489
She didn't know anything
about love.

1225
01:05:13.160 --> 01:05:14.321
<b><font color="#408080">CULLEN:</font></b> Mrs. Adler...

1226
01:05:14.480 --> 01:05:16.403
in January of 1998...

1227
01:05:16.600 --> 01:05:19.570
didn't Diane and young
Mr. Riva run away together?

1228
01:05:19.720 --> 01:05:20.721
He coerced her.

1229
01:05:20.880 --> 01:05:22.325
- And where did they go?
- Vermont.

1230
01:05:22.400 --> 01:05:23.890
And you called the police,
didn't you?

1231
01:05:23.960 --> 01:05:25.416
- Because he kidnapped her?
- Yes. Yes.

1232
01:05:25.440 --> 01:05:27.920
And where did the police
find young Mr. Riva and Diane?

1233
01:05:28.080 --> 01:05:29.445
I told you. Vermont.

1234
01:05:29.600 --> 01:05:31.364
Stowe, Vermont. Wasn't it?

1235
01:05:31.520 --> 01:05:32.521
A resort town.

1236
01:05:32.600 --> 01:05:34.682
Stowe Mountain.

1237
01:05:34.840 --> 01:05:36.922
He took her skiing.

1238
01:05:37.600 --> 01:05:38.816
You see,
kidnappers don't usually

1239
01:05:38.840 --> 01:05:40.251
take their victims skiing.

1240
01:05:40.400 --> 01:05:44.246
But this is what Paul did...

1241
01:05:44.400 --> 01:05:47.085
because he and Diane
were in love.

1242
01:05:47.160 --> 01:05:48.161
No.

1243
01:05:48.240 --> 01:05:49.480
And when they returned,

1244
01:05:49.560 --> 01:05:51.483
you pressed kidnapping charges.

1245
01:05:51.640 --> 01:05:54.803
You filed a lawsuit
against his parents...

1246
01:05:54.960 --> 01:05:57.008
until Paul stopped
calling Diane.

1247
01:05:57.160 --> 01:05:58.491
- Didn't you?
- Yes.

1248
01:05:58.680 --> 01:06:02.287
And Diane never heard from
or saw Paul Riva again.

1249
01:06:02.360 --> 01:06:03.361
Did she?

1250
01:06:03.480 --> 01:06:05.209
Not to my knowledge.

1251
01:06:07.520 --> 01:06:09.090
And how did she take it?

1252
01:06:10.000 --> 01:06:12.367
She was upset for a while.

1253
01:06:12.520 --> 01:06:13.681
She lost focus.

1254
01:06:14.760 --> 01:06:16.603
She lost focus.

1255
01:06:16.760 --> 01:06:19.331
Mrs. Adler,
in March of 1999...

1256
01:06:19.480 --> 01:06:22.131
didn't Diane Adler attempt
to take her own life?

1257
01:06:22.280 --> 01:06:24.248
This episode was minor.
It was nothing.

1258
01:06:24.400 --> 01:06:26.801
I have the hospital report
in my hand.

1259
01:06:27.000 --> 01:06:28.331
It was nothing.

1260
01:06:28.480 --> 01:06:30.687
Diane was not like
regular people.

1261
01:06:30.880 --> 01:06:32.086
She was extraordinary.

1262
01:06:32.240 --> 01:06:33.571
And extraordinary people

1263
01:06:33.640 --> 01:06:36.246
come with singular issues
and needs.

1264
01:06:36.400 --> 01:06:39.529
You have no idea
the capability she possessed.

1265
01:06:39.960 --> 01:06:41.405
One in a billion.

1266
01:06:41.600 --> 01:06:44.365
And you would say, "Fine,
let's throw that away...

1267
01:06:44.560 --> 01:06:48.610
"so the boy who cuts our yard
can make a sexual conquest."

1268
01:06:48.760 --> 01:06:50.489
Maybe before you make
that decision,

1269
01:06:50.560 --> 01:06:52.085
you stand in my shoes.

1270
01:06:52.240 --> 01:06:54.686
I had responsibilities
which went beyond

1271
01:06:54.760 --> 01:06:56.171
the mother-daughter
relationship.

1272
01:06:56.360 --> 01:06:58.362
The greatest discoveries

1273
01:06:58.440 --> 01:07:00.283
which have improved life
on this planet...

1274
01:07:00.440 --> 01:07:03.683
have come from minds
rarer than radium.

1275
01:07:03.840 --> 01:07:06.491
Without them,
we'd still be crawling in mud.

1276
01:07:06.640 --> 01:07:09.211
And for your information,
counselor...

1277
01:07:09.360 --> 01:07:12.170
a year after this incident
with this boy...

1278
01:07:12.320 --> 01:07:14.561
Diane thanked me
for my intervention.

1279
01:07:14.960 --> 01:07:17.486
She realized she'd made
a mistake and she thanked me.

1280
01:07:17.640 --> 01:07:19.483
You see, Diane understood
she was accountable

1281
01:07:19.560 --> 01:07:20.766
for the gift she'd been given.

1282
01:07:20.920 --> 01:07:22.126
And she didn't shy from it.

1283
01:07:22.280 --> 01:07:25.204
And I think, if she were
here today, Mr. Attorney...

1284
01:07:25.360 --> 01:07:27.840
she would refute
your baseless insinuations...

1285
01:07:28.000 --> 01:07:29.684
that she would give up
her brilliant future

1286
01:07:29.760 --> 01:07:30.841
and take her own life...

1287
01:07:31.000 --> 01:07:34.607
just because Mommy didn't
get her a little red wagon.

1288
01:07:37.080 --> 01:07:38.650
No more questions.

1289
01:07:47.600 --> 01:07:49.762
<b><font color="#808000">(INAUDIBLE)</font></b>

1290
01:08:14.760 --> 01:08:15.841
<i><b><font color="#408080">HIGHSMITH:</font></b> Mr. Adler...</i>

1291
01:08:16.920 --> 01:08:18.729
where are you
currently employed?

1292
01:08:18.880 --> 01:08:19.881
I repair boats.

1293
01:08:19.960 --> 01:08:21.769
Oh, really? At which marina?

1294
01:08:21.920 --> 01:08:23.809
I don't work at a marina.
I freelance.

1295
01:08:23.960 --> 01:08:25.962
So, safe to say,
no health insurance.

1296
01:08:26.160 --> 01:08:27.161
No.

1297
01:08:27.520 --> 01:08:29.376
<b><font color="#408080">HIGHSMITH:</font></b> About a week before
your sister took her life...

1298
01:08:29.400 --> 01:08:31.004
what were you doing
for a living then?

1299
01:08:31.600 --> 01:08:32.761
I was a teacher.

1300
01:08:33.040 --> 01:08:34.576
<b><font color="#408080">HIGHSMITH:</font></b> You're being
modest, aren't you?

1301
01:08:34.600 --> 01:08:36.568
You were a professor
at Boston University...

1302
01:08:36.720 --> 01:08:37.801
isn't that right?

1303
01:08:37.880 --> 01:08:39.848
Yes, well, Assistant Professor.

1304
01:08:40.000 --> 01:08:41.411
And what'd you teach?

1305
01:08:41.480 --> 01:08:42.481
Philosophy.

1306
01:08:42.560 --> 01:08:44.483
Truth and logic.
That sort of thing.

1307
01:08:45.440 --> 01:08:47.681
Your attorney said
that the primary reason...

1308
01:08:47.840 --> 01:08:50.320
that you took Mary is because
it was what your sister...

1309
01:08:50.520 --> 01:08:52.841
would have wanted you to do.
Is that the truth?

1310
01:08:53.000 --> 01:08:54.001
Yes.

1311
01:08:54.480 --> 01:08:56.200
So Diane had visited
Pinellas County before?

1312
01:08:56.960 --> 01:08:57.961
No.

1313
01:08:58.040 --> 01:08:59.176
Indicated she wanted
her daughter

1314
01:08:59.200 --> 01:09:00.361
uprooted and moved here?

1315
01:09:00.440 --> 01:09:02.204
- No.
- No.

1316
01:09:02.360 --> 01:09:04.681
So, you decided to bring
Mary here, didn't you?

1317
01:09:06.160 --> 01:09:07.400
Did Diane have a problem

1318
01:09:07.480 --> 01:09:09.160
with your health plan
at Boston University?

1319
01:09:09.200 --> 01:09:10.247
Not to my knowledge.

1320
01:09:10.320 --> 01:09:11.416
Do you think she would want
her daughter

1321
01:09:11.440 --> 01:09:12.576
to have access to healthcare?

1322
01:09:12.600 --> 01:09:13.487
Of course.

1323
01:09:13.560 --> 01:09:16.245
So what do you do,
when little Mary gets sick?

1324
01:09:16.400 --> 01:09:17.845
You repair a doctor's boat?

1325
01:09:18.000 --> 01:09:19.809
- Objection.
- <b><font color="#408080">NICHOLS:</font></b> Sustained.

1326
01:09:20.680 --> 01:09:21.841
Mary has been identified...

1327
01:09:22.000 --> 01:09:24.207
as a math prodigy.

1328
01:09:24.360 --> 01:09:26.567
But her formal math education
under your care...

1329
01:09:26.760 --> 01:09:28.922
she's learning at the
first grade level, isn't she?

1330
01:09:29.000 --> 01:09:30.047
<b><font color="#408080">FRANK:</font></b> Yes.

1331
01:09:30.120 --> 01:09:32.646
<b><font color="#408080">HIGHSMITH:</font></b> And you turned down
a scholarship at the Oaks...

1332
01:09:32.720 --> 01:09:36.042
a school specializing in the
education of gifted children?

1333
01:09:36.120 --> 01:09:37.121
Yes.

1334
01:09:37.200 --> 01:09:39.248
And Diane,
she'd be fine with that?

1335
01:09:41.080 --> 01:09:42.241
I couldn't say.

1336
01:09:42.400 --> 01:09:44.176
<b><font color="#408080">HIGHSMITH:</font></b> Prior to Mrs. Adler
giving her one,

1337
01:09:44.200 --> 01:09:45.281
did Mary have a computer?

1338
01:09:45.360 --> 01:09:46.407
She used mine.

1339
01:09:46.480 --> 01:09:48.642
Mr. Adler, does Diane's
daughter have her own bedroom?

1340
01:09:48.720 --> 01:09:49.767
<b><font color="#408080">FRANK:</font></b> No.

1341
01:09:49.840 --> 01:09:51.536
<b><font color="#408080">HIGHSMITH:</font></b> Does she sleep
in a bed that you bought...

1342
01:09:51.560 --> 01:09:53.005
- in a secondhand shop?
- Yes.

1343
01:09:53.160 --> 01:09:55.686
So, the truth is, Mr. Adler,

1344
01:09:55.840 --> 01:09:57.330
that you didn't come down here

1345
01:09:57.400 --> 01:09:58.970
because your sister
wanted you to...

1346
01:09:59.040 --> 01:10:00.256
and you certainly
didn't come here

1347
01:10:00.280 --> 01:10:01.440
because it was good for Mary.

1348
01:10:01.480 --> 01:10:03.289
No, it was personal.

1349
01:10:03.440 --> 01:10:05.124
Diane was a star.
You weren't.

1350
01:10:05.280 --> 01:10:06.964
Diane got the attention.
You didn't.

1351
01:10:07.120 --> 01:10:08.531
And over the years,
you got angry.

1352
01:10:08.640 --> 01:10:09.721
And here comes Mary.

1353
01:10:09.800 --> 01:10:11.848
- What a great way to get even.
- <b><font color="#808000">(SIGHS)</font></b>

1354
01:10:12.000 --> 01:10:13.604
You've uprooted that little girl

1355
01:10:13.680 --> 01:10:15.364
and brought her here
for one reason only.

1356
01:10:15.560 --> 01:10:17.403
To do harm to your mother.

1357
01:10:17.480 --> 01:10:18.720
You blamed her for your sister.

1358
01:10:18.760 --> 01:10:19.841
No, I don't.

1359
01:10:19.920 --> 01:10:21.576
And Mary to you is just a pawn
in all this. Isn't she?

1360
01:10:21.600 --> 01:10:23.648
Diane wanted Mary...

1361
01:10:23.800 --> 01:10:25.131
to be a kid.

1362
01:10:26.560 --> 01:10:28.608
She wanted her to have a life.

1363
01:10:30.160 --> 01:10:31.889
She wanted her
to have friends...

1364
01:10:32.680 --> 01:10:34.284
and to play...

1365
01:10:35.560 --> 01:10:36.607
and to be happy.

1366
01:10:36.760 --> 01:10:38.922
Do you realize the consequences

1367
01:10:39.000 --> 01:10:41.002
of boredom for a gifted child,
Mr. Adler?

1368
01:10:41.160 --> 01:10:43.288
They become resentful
and sullen.

1369
01:10:43.440 --> 01:10:44.487
Mary's not an angry kid.

1370
01:10:44.560 --> 01:10:45.641
<b><font color="#408080">HIGHSMITH:</font></b> Really?

1371
01:10:45.760 --> 01:10:47.728
Did she not attack a child
on the school bus?

1372
01:10:48.400 --> 01:10:49.526
<b><font color="#408080">FRANK:</font></b> A 12-year-old tripped

1373
01:10:49.640 --> 01:10:51.016
a 7-year-old
and she came to his defense.

1374
01:10:51.040 --> 01:10:52.963
- Did she break the boy's nose?
- Yes.

1375
01:10:53.120 --> 01:10:57.125
On October 16th of last year
were you arrested for assault?

1376
01:10:57.280 --> 01:10:58.520
Oh, my God.

1377
01:10:58.800 --> 01:11:00.840
<b><font color="#408080">NICHOLS:</font></b> You have to answer
the question, Mr. Adler.

1378
01:11:02.800 --> 01:11:06.122
A drunk idiot attacked me
and I defended myself.

1379
01:11:06.320 --> 01:11:07.376
What does that have to do
with anything?

1380
01:11:07.400 --> 01:11:08.680
Did you spend the night in jail?

1381
01:11:09.440 --> 01:11:10.521
Yeah.

1382
01:11:10.600 --> 01:11:12.648
You are in way
over your head here.

1383
01:11:12.800 --> 01:11:14.245
You're depriving that girl.

1384
01:11:14.400 --> 01:11:15.696
You're gambling
with her future...

1385
01:11:15.720 --> 01:11:17.776
and now you're being presented
with an opportunity...

1386
01:11:17.800 --> 01:11:18.976
to do right by her.
Jump at it.

1387
01:11:19.000 --> 01:11:20.336
<b><font color="#408080">CULLEN:</font></b> Does counsel
have a question for my client?

1388
01:11:20.360 --> 01:11:21.771
Take the high road,
Mr. Adler...

1389
01:11:21.880 --> 01:11:23.016
before she is
irreparably damaged.

1390
01:11:23.040 --> 01:11:24.136
<b><font color="#408080">CULLEN:</font></b> Does counsel
have a question...

1391
01:11:24.160 --> 01:11:25.176
- for my client or not?
- Yes, sir.

1392
01:11:25.200 --> 01:11:26.850
I do have the question.

1393
01:11:28.520 --> 01:11:31.000
Tell us, is your
continued guardianship...

1394
01:11:31.160 --> 01:11:34.323
really in the best interest
of this little girl?

1395
01:11:41.240 --> 01:11:42.366
Yes.

1396
01:11:43.040 --> 01:11:44.041
Yes, it is.

1397
01:11:44.640 --> 01:11:46.563
<b><font color="#408080">HIGHSMITH:</font></b> I have
no further questions.

1398
01:11:54.240 --> 01:11:55.416
<b><font color="#408080">MARY:</font></b> Research
and Development...

1399
01:11:55.440 --> 01:11:57.044
has come up
with a brand new Fred cheer.

1400
01:11:57.200 --> 01:12:00.170
Fred personally asked me
if I would tell you it.

1401
01:12:00.320 --> 01:12:02.209
Let's hear it.

1402
01:12:02.360 --> 01:12:05.569
<i>S-O-C-K I-T
Sock it to me, Freddy...</i>

1403
01:12:05.720 --> 01:12:08.041
<i>Sock it.</i>

1404
01:12:08.120 --> 01:12:09.485
<b><font color="#808000">(VOCALIZING)</font></b>

1405
01:12:09.560 --> 01:12:11.881
<i>Sock it.</i>

1406
01:12:12.000 --> 01:12:14.128
<b><font color="#808000">(VOCALIZING)</font></b>

1407
01:12:15.600 --> 01:12:17.170
Tell R and D, they got a winner.

1408
01:12:17.320 --> 01:12:19.482
That's exactly what I said.

1409
01:12:20.680 --> 01:12:24.127
<b><font color="#408080">MARY:</font></b> <i>S-O-C-K I-T
Sock it to me, Freddy</i>

1410
01:12:26.680 --> 01:12:28.648
<b><font color="#408080">CULLEN:</font></b> They wanna cut a deal.

1411
01:12:30.880 --> 01:12:32.803
Highsmith called me
first thing this morning.

1412
01:12:32.960 --> 01:12:34.560
Why would he do that?
I don't understand.

1413
01:12:34.600 --> 01:12:35.965
In the middle of...

1414
01:12:36.160 --> 01:12:38.561
Just listen.
Listen to me, man.

1415
01:12:41.240 --> 01:12:44.608
Mary would be put
in a fostering situation.

1416
01:12:44.760 --> 01:12:45.886
No.

1417
01:12:46.040 --> 01:12:47.256
- Come on, Frank.
- Cullen, I told you no.

1418
01:12:47.280 --> 01:12:48.645
It wouldn't be
a court-appointed

1419
01:12:48.760 --> 01:12:50.046
foster family, Frank.

1420
01:12:50.200 --> 01:12:51.616
- Hey! Drop it!
- We could cherry-pick...

1421
01:12:51.640 --> 01:12:52.766
from the cream of the crop.

1422
01:12:52.840 --> 01:12:54.490
Mutually approved.

1423
01:12:55.000 --> 01:12:56.650
Just hear me out.

1424
01:12:56.720 --> 01:12:57.767
Tampa.

1425
01:12:57.840 --> 01:12:59.888
I insisted, and they agreed.

1426
01:13:00.040 --> 01:13:02.646
No more than a half an hour
from your front door.

1427
01:13:02.800 --> 01:13:04.723
Now, she would attend the Oaks.

1428
01:13:04.880 --> 01:13:06.530
It's a great school.

1429
01:13:06.680 --> 01:13:08.320
And visiting rights
for the grandmother...

1430
01:13:08.440 --> 01:13:09.885
but she was gonna
get that anyway.

1431
01:13:10.040 --> 01:13:12.042
Right? And on her
12th birthday, Frank,

1432
01:13:12.120 --> 01:13:13.406
she can go back into court...

1433
01:13:13.560 --> 01:13:14.641
- No.
- And decide then...

1434
01:13:14.720 --> 01:13:15.896
where she wants to live
and with who.

1435
01:13:15.920 --> 01:13:16.807
I don't understand this.

1436
01:13:16.880 --> 01:13:18.216
I thought you were supposed
to be on my team.

1437
01:13:18.240 --> 01:13:19.416
Why are you bringing me
this deal?

1438
01:13:19.440 --> 01:13:21.442
Other than
I'm required to by law?

1439
01:13:21.600 --> 01:13:23.011
You like this deal?

1440
01:13:24.640 --> 01:13:26.165
I love this deal.

1441
01:13:43.920 --> 01:13:45.604
They think they're gonna lose.

1442
01:13:46.160 --> 01:13:47.605
Yeah.

1443
01:13:47.760 --> 01:13:48.886
They do.

1444
01:13:50.320 --> 01:13:51.810
You think we're gonna lose.

1445
01:13:52.080 --> 01:13:53.161
Yes.

1446
01:13:53.360 --> 01:13:55.044
I do, Frank.

1447
01:14:01.080 --> 01:14:02.206
<b><font color="#808000">(CULLEN EXHALES)</font></b>

1448
01:14:02.760 --> 01:14:05.331
I gotta go put my kids to bed.

1449
01:14:06.960 --> 01:14:09.201
I'll do whatever
you want me to do.

1450
01:14:10.000 --> 01:14:13.402
But if we leave this
up to that judge, Nichols...

1451
01:14:13.560 --> 01:14:15.562
he's old school, Frank.

1452
01:14:15.760 --> 01:14:17.842
Does he like your mother? No.

1453
01:14:18.000 --> 01:14:19.331
Does he like her income?

1454
01:14:19.480 --> 01:14:22.529
Does he like her health plan?
Does he like her home?

1455
01:14:22.680 --> 01:14:24.648
You better believe it.

1456
01:14:24.800 --> 01:14:26.529
I've been in his courtroom.

1457
01:14:26.680 --> 01:14:28.250
A hundred times.

1458
01:14:29.400 --> 01:14:31.209
And if it's a coin toss...

1459
01:14:31.400 --> 01:14:32.526
Look at me.

1460
01:14:35.480 --> 01:14:38.245
If it's a coin toss...

1461
01:14:38.400 --> 01:14:42.325
that old boy is going to
side with the money.

1462
01:14:43.160 --> 01:14:45.162
So, do me a favor, Frank.

1463
01:14:45.320 --> 01:14:48.051
Just meet the family.
See how it feels.

1464
01:14:48.200 --> 01:14:49.565
It's all I ask.

1465
01:15:03.840 --> 01:15:09.961
<b><font color="#408080">WOMAN:</font></b> <b><font color="#808000">(SINGING)</font></b>
<i><font color="#D900D9">♪</font> Fly away, little pretty bird <font color="#D900D9">♪</font></i>

1466
01:15:11.920 --> 01:15:16.562
<i><font color="#D900D9">♪</font> Fly, fly away... <font color="#D900D9">♪</font></i>

1467
01:15:17.600 --> 01:15:20.046
Pool. Florida.

1468
01:15:20.880 --> 01:15:23.360
That dock
is on the Intercoastal.

1469
01:15:23.520 --> 01:15:25.284
Which I guess is obvious...

1470
01:15:25.440 --> 01:15:27.442
since we're on the Intercoastal.

1471
01:15:30.040 --> 01:15:32.281
Added this desk,
so she could do her homework.

1472
01:15:32.440 --> 01:15:33.965
Yeah.

1473
01:15:34.160 --> 01:15:36.447
And this would be her room.

1474
01:15:38.040 --> 01:15:44.400
<i>I see in your eyes a promise</i>

1475
01:15:45.120 --> 01:15:48.124
So, while I'm trying to
sell you on the house...

1476
01:15:48.280 --> 01:15:49.964
and us...

1477
01:15:50.120 --> 01:15:51.690
I just don't want you to think

1478
01:15:51.760 --> 01:15:53.489
that we're
completely insensitive...

1479
01:15:53.640 --> 01:15:55.324
to what you're feeling.

1480
01:16:06.080 --> 01:16:08.560
You guys gonna be okay
with a cat?

1481
01:16:10.440 --> 01:16:12.204
Absolutely.

1482
01:16:14.400 --> 01:16:18.962
<i><font color="#D900D9">♪</font> Love's own tender flames <font color="#D900D9">♪</font></i>

1483
01:16:19.320 --> 01:16:21.482
<i><font color="#D900D9">♪</font> Warm this meeting <font color="#D900D9">♪</font></i>

1484
01:16:23.000 --> 01:16:29.007
<i><font color="#D900D9">♪</font> And love's tender song
you will sing <font color="#D900D9">♪</font></i>

1485
01:16:31.120 --> 01:16:34.283
<i><font color="#D900D9">♪</font> But fly away... <font color="#D900D9">♪</font></i>

1486
01:16:34.400 --> 01:16:37.688
I've been thinking a lot
about the word "compromise."

1487
01:16:37.840 --> 01:16:41.322
On one hand,
good challenging school...

1488
01:16:41.480 --> 01:16:42.811
on the other...

1489
01:16:43.280 --> 01:16:44.645
foster people.

1490
01:16:44.800 --> 01:16:46.404
They can watch sitcoms with her.

1491
01:16:46.560 --> 01:16:48.483
Take her to Olive Garden.

1492
01:16:48.640 --> 01:16:51.291
Teach her
to say "irregardless."

1493
01:16:51.440 --> 01:16:53.169
The only saving grace,
I suppose,

1494
01:16:53.240 --> 01:16:55.288
is she's better off
than she was.

1495
01:16:58.160 --> 01:16:59.366
Well...

1496
01:17:01.800 --> 01:17:03.404
Goodbye, Frank.

1497
01:17:16.720 --> 01:17:18.404
<b><font color="#808000">(SIGHS)</font></b> Hey.

1498
01:17:18.560 --> 01:17:20.130
<i>Come on.</i>

1499
01:17:20.320 --> 01:17:21.970
I'm only 25 minutes away.

1500
01:17:22.120 --> 01:17:24.168
<b><font color="#808000">(CRYING)</font></b>
Please don't leave me here.

1501
01:17:24.320 --> 01:17:25.731
- Please.
- No.

1502
01:17:27.280 --> 01:17:28.327
Look, you're going to

1503
01:17:28.400 --> 01:17:29.811
a brand new school,
a better school.

1504
01:17:29.960 --> 01:17:31.724
I don't want to.

1505
01:17:31.880 --> 01:17:33.041
I want my crummy school.

1506
01:17:33.200 --> 01:17:34.440
- And you got Fred.
- Please.

1507
01:17:34.600 --> 01:17:35.936
And once a month
you can come back.

1508
01:17:35.960 --> 01:17:37.096
You can stay with me
and Roberta.

1509
01:17:37.120 --> 01:17:39.168
I want you and Roberta now!

1510
01:17:39.320 --> 01:17:41.084
And in a few years,
if you want...

1511
01:17:41.240 --> 01:17:42.844
you can come back.
You can live with me.

1512
01:17:43.320 --> 01:17:44.685
I wanna stay with you.

1513
01:17:44.840 --> 01:17:46.330
Frank, you promised me.

1514
01:17:46.480 --> 01:17:48.482
- Come on. Don't do this.
- You promised me.

1515
01:17:48.680 --> 01:17:49.806
I know.

1516
01:17:50.560 --> 01:17:51.641
I know.

1517
01:17:52.240 --> 01:17:54.891
Please, Frank.
Why are you leaving me?

1518
01:17:55.040 --> 01:17:58.010
Because the court said
I had to. You know this.

1519
01:17:58.160 --> 01:18:00.003
We've discussed this ad naus...

1520
01:18:02.200 --> 01:18:05.090
We've discussed this. Come on.

1521
01:18:06.920 --> 01:18:08.570
- Come on, please.
- No!

1522
01:18:08.640 --> 01:18:09.766
- Hey! Mary.
- No!

1523
01:18:09.840 --> 01:18:10.921
Hey, Mary.

1524
01:18:11.040 --> 01:18:12.166
No! No!

1525
01:18:12.320 --> 01:18:13.446
Sweetheart, you know what?

1526
01:18:13.600 --> 01:18:14.681
No!

1527
01:18:14.880 --> 01:18:16.291
There's no easy way to do this.

1528
01:18:16.440 --> 01:18:18.249
Not gonna be as long
as you think, honey.

1529
01:18:18.400 --> 01:18:19.561
- Frank!
- You're gonna see.

1530
01:18:19.720 --> 01:18:21.006
<b><font color="#408080">MARY:</font></b> Frank!

1531
01:18:21.280 --> 01:18:23.203
Don't leave me!

1532
01:18:23.360 --> 01:18:25.442
- I know this is hard.
- Please!

1533
01:18:25.600 --> 01:18:28.001
No! Get your hands off of me!

1534
01:18:28.160 --> 01:18:30.128
- Frank! Please!
- I know it's hard.

1535
01:18:30.280 --> 01:18:32.089
- Come back!
- Mary! I know.

1536
01:18:32.240 --> 01:18:33.446
No!

1537
01:18:33.640 --> 01:18:35.324
Frank!

1538
01:18:35.800 --> 01:18:37.404
Don't go!

1539
01:18:38.760 --> 01:18:40.410
I need you!

1540
01:18:40.560 --> 01:18:42.881
- Fred needs you!
- <b><font color="#808000">(STARTS ENGINE)</font></b>

1541
01:18:43.200 --> 01:18:44.440
You promised me!

1542
01:18:51.200 --> 01:18:52.247
No!

1543
01:19:06.720 --> 01:19:08.290
<b><font color="#808000">(SIGHS)</font></b> Huh.

1544
01:19:32.040 --> 01:19:33.451
<b><font color="#408080">BONNIE:</font></b> Hey, down there.

1545
01:19:36.080 --> 01:19:37.760
<b><font color="#408080">FRANK:</font></b> I don't know
which mistake is worse.

1546
01:19:37.800 --> 01:19:39.370
Designing a water pump
that leaks...

1547
01:19:39.520 --> 01:19:42.330
or putting it somewhere
no human being can reach.

1548
01:19:44.160 --> 01:19:45.241
<b><font color="#808000">(FRANK GRUNTS)</font></b>

1549
01:19:46.360 --> 01:19:48.886
How do you design something
you know is gonna fail?

1550
01:19:50.280 --> 01:19:53.284
Gotta be devious
or clueless, right?

1551
01:19:58.640 --> 01:20:00.324
After the first few weeks,
I knew...

1552
01:20:00.520 --> 01:20:02.522
I had to find
a real family for her.

1553
01:20:05.680 --> 01:20:08.126
I was in way over my head.

1554
01:20:11.560 --> 01:20:15.007
And every day, I'd say,
"Today's the day...

1555
01:20:15.200 --> 01:20:17.043
"I'm takin' her
to child services."

1556
01:20:17.200 --> 01:20:19.965
And every day,
she'd do something just...

1557
01:20:23.520 --> 01:20:26.000
so unbelievably cool.

1558
01:20:27.480 --> 01:20:30.165
Her little personality
was exploding.

1559
01:20:32.080 --> 01:20:33.764
She was funny.

1560
01:20:34.720 --> 01:20:37.451
And she was angry, and
she was happy, and she was...

1561
01:20:38.600 --> 01:20:40.045
sad.

1562
01:20:41.360 --> 01:20:42.441
And was cute.

1563
01:20:44.280 --> 01:20:46.442
Just so damn entertaining.

1564
01:20:46.600 --> 01:20:48.443
And so I kept her.

1565
01:20:48.600 --> 01:20:50.125
Not that
that's in her best interest.

1566
01:20:50.320 --> 01:20:52.926
Not that I'm capable
of raising a child.

1567
01:20:53.080 --> 01:20:54.680
A child that might still
have a mother...

1568
01:20:54.720 --> 01:20:57.200
if I had taken the time to
notice that she needed me.

1569
01:20:58.440 --> 01:20:59.487
<b><font color="#808000">(GRUNTS)</font></b>

1570
01:20:59.560 --> 01:21:02.609
And now,
six and a half years later...

1571
01:21:02.760 --> 01:21:04.649
I finally got her
to the foster family,

1572
01:21:04.720 --> 01:21:06.051
and you know what?

1573
01:21:06.200 --> 01:21:08.282
It was great. She loved it.

1574
01:21:08.920 --> 01:21:10.968
I thought
it would be a nightmare...

1575
01:21:11.120 --> 01:21:12.610
of abandonment and betrayal.

1576
01:21:12.720 --> 01:21:14.882
But, turns out
it was a huge success.

1577
01:21:16.160 --> 01:21:17.241
I'm a fucking hero.

1578
01:21:17.840 --> 01:21:18.966
<b><font color="#808000">(CRASHING)</font></b>

1579
01:21:23.520 --> 01:21:25.204
- Frank...
- No, Bonnie.

1580
01:21:26.040 --> 01:21:27.804
I appreciate
everything you've done.

1581
01:21:27.960 --> 01:21:29.200
Just"

1582
01:21:48.320 --> 01:21:50.084
- Hey.
- Hi, Frank.

1583
01:21:52.320 --> 01:21:54.368
We're having a little bit
of a problem in there.

1584
01:21:54.720 --> 01:21:55.721
What?

1585
01:21:55.880 --> 01:21:57.848
Uh, Mary's fine...

1586
01:21:58.000 --> 01:21:59.889
but she's having
a little bit of a meltdown.

1587
01:22:00.760 --> 01:22:02.922
Right. Let me talk to her.

1588
01:22:03.080 --> 01:22:04.491
No, hang on.

1589
01:22:05.200 --> 01:22:07.646
That's not a good idea.

1590
01:22:08.000 --> 01:22:11.129
Your visit here is the reason
for the meltdown.

1591
01:22:11.400 --> 01:22:12.526
What?

1592
01:22:13.880 --> 01:22:15.882
She don't wanna see you.

1593
01:22:21.120 --> 01:22:22.645
I'm sorry, man.

1594
01:22:23.400 --> 01:22:25.050
This is predictable.

1595
01:22:25.200 --> 01:22:27.726
You know, she needs time.

1596
01:22:33.160 --> 01:22:34.810
Just give me five minutes
with her.

1597
01:22:35.360 --> 01:22:36.691
I let you inside...

1598
01:22:36.840 --> 01:22:38.569
then we're gonna
betray her trust

1599
01:22:38.680 --> 01:22:40.170
and we have
an even bigger problem.

1600
01:23:44.920 --> 01:23:46.922
<b><font color="#808000">(CHILDREN CHATTERING)</font></b>

1601
01:23:56.480 --> 01:23:57.811
Oh...

1602
01:24:13.000 --> 01:24:15.048
<b><font color="#808000">(CELL PHONE RINGING)</font></b>

1603
01:24:24.920 --> 01:24:26.968
<b><font color="#808000">(SIGHS)</font></b>

1604
01:24:38.480 --> 01:24:40.528
<b><font color="#808000">(CELL PHONE CHIMING)</font></b>

1605
01:25:05.720 --> 01:25:07.131
- Hi.
- Hi! Can we help you?

1606
01:25:07.240 --> 01:25:09.766
Yeah. Do you have a one-eyed
orange cat named Fred?

1607
01:25:11.680 --> 01:25:13.170
We have a one-eyed cat,

1608
01:25:13.240 --> 01:25:14.560
but I don't know
what his name is.

1609
01:25:14.680 --> 01:25:16.011
Where?

1610
01:25:16.160 --> 01:25:18.561
I don't know.
Today was his last day.

1611
01:25:21.080 --> 01:25:22.650
Hey, you can't go back there.

1612
01:25:24.000 --> 01:25:25.923
<b><font color="#808000">(DOG BARKING)</font></b>

1613
01:25:26.280 --> 01:25:27.281
Hey.

1614
01:25:29.720 --> 01:25:32.166
Whoa, whoa, whoa,
what are you doing back here?

1615
01:25:32.760 --> 01:25:34.040
- <b><font color="#808000">(SIGHS <i>IN</i> RELIEF)</font></b>
- <b><font color="#808000">(CAT MEOWS)</font></b>

1616
01:25:36.400 --> 01:25:38.528
- I told him he couldn't.
- <b><font color="#808000">(SIGHS)</font></b>

1617
01:25:42.880 --> 01:25:43.881
Hey, Fred.

1618
01:25:44.000 --> 01:25:45.001
<b><font color="#808000">(MEOWS)</font></b>

1619
01:25:47.160 --> 01:25:48.685
Who brought this cat in?

1620
01:25:48.840 --> 01:25:51.207
Some guy. He said
it was an allergy issue.

1621
01:25:52.680 --> 01:25:53.886
<b><font color="#808000">(SIGHS)</font></b>

1622
01:25:54.360 --> 01:25:55.964
Evelyn.

1623
01:26:00.640 --> 01:26:02.688
<b><font color="#808000">(CATS MEOWING)</font></b>

1624
01:26:38.120 --> 01:26:39.406
- Frank!
- Where's Evelyn?

1625
01:26:39.560 --> 01:26:40.925
Evelyn? You mean your mother?

1626
01:26:41.080 --> 01:26:42.650
- Yes.
- Kevin.

1627
01:26:42.840 --> 01:26:44.171
Evelyn is in the guest house.

1628
01:26:44.320 --> 01:26:46.402
Yeah, listen. I'm sorry, man.

1629
01:26:46.560 --> 01:26:47.846
Frank, let me tell you,

1630
01:26:47.920 --> 01:26:49.888
she was so helpful
with the tutors.

1631
01:26:50.040 --> 01:26:51.280
- Mary was so distant.
- Hey!

1632
01:26:51.440 --> 01:26:52.656
Look, we know
what you're up against.

1633
01:26:52.680 --> 01:26:53.696
Do you want your life back?

1634
01:26:53.720 --> 01:26:54.720
Go back inside.

1635
01:26:59.800 --> 01:27:01.006
What are you doing here?

1636
01:27:03.480 --> 01:27:04.845
Come on, Mary.
We're goin' home.

1637
01:27:05.000 --> 01:27:07.526
- No.
- You're trespassing!

1638
01:27:09.440 --> 01:27:10.805
You need to look at this.

1639
01:27:11.400 --> 01:27:12.400
Let's go, Mary.

1640
01:27:12.440 --> 01:27:13.601
- Let's move. Come on.
- No!

1641
01:27:13.680 --> 01:27:14.761
- Hey! Mary.
- Mary!

1642
01:27:14.920 --> 01:27:16.001
Mary!

1643
01:27:16.200 --> 01:27:18.487
- It's okay. I got her.
- Uh-uh!

1644
01:27:20.360 --> 01:27:22.522
Think again, lady.

1645
01:27:45.200 --> 01:27:46.326
Mary!

1646
01:27:46.480 --> 01:27:48.642
Mary! Hey, stop it.

1647
01:27:48.800 --> 01:27:50.165
No! Let me go!

1648
01:27:50.320 --> 01:27:51.401
No!

1649
01:27:51.560 --> 01:27:53.244
- No! No!
'Stop, Mary!

1650
01:27:53.400 --> 01:27:54.845
- Stop. Stop
- No!

1651
01:27:55.040 --> 01:27:56.485
- Come here.
- You lied to me!

1652
01:27:56.640 --> 01:27:57.641
I know!

1653
01:27:57.760 --> 01:28:00.286
- No! No!
'Stop! Stop, Stop!

1654
01:28:00.520 --> 01:28:01.806
- <b><font color="#808000">(SOBBING)</font></b>
- I'm sorry.

1655
01:28:02.000 --> 01:28:04.606
I made a mistake.
I'm so sorry.

1656
01:28:04.800 --> 01:28:05.926
You promised me.

1657
01:28:06.080 --> 01:28:07.161
I know.

1658
01:28:07.320 --> 01:28:08.481
You promised.

1659
01:28:08.560 --> 01:28:10.847
I know. I'm sorry.

1660
01:28:12.160 --> 01:28:13.605
You said you wouldn't leave me.

1661
01:28:13.880 --> 01:28:15.405
I made a mistake.

1662
01:28:16.440 --> 01:28:18.647
<b><font color="#808000">(SIGHS)</font></b> I'm sorry.

1663
01:28:21.840 --> 01:28:23.808
I was so sad!

1664
01:28:25.080 --> 01:28:26.161
I missed you.

1665
01:28:26.360 --> 01:28:27.964
I missed you, too.

1666
01:28:29.160 --> 01:28:30.889
They took Fred.

1667
01:28:31.040 --> 01:28:33.566
No. It's okay.

1668
01:28:33.720 --> 01:28:36.485
- I got him. He's home.
- What?

1669
01:28:36.640 --> 01:28:39.166
He's at the apartment
right now. I got him.

1670
01:28:39.840 --> 01:28:41.046
For real?

1671
01:28:41.160 --> 01:28:44.767
Yeah. He's home kicking around
his ping pong ball.

1672
01:28:45.840 --> 01:28:47.888
Why did you leave me there?

1673
01:28:49.440 --> 01:28:51.681
Because I thought
I was bad for you.

1674
01:28:52.800 --> 01:28:54.325
And then it dawned on me.

1675
01:28:54.480 --> 01:28:56.926
If Mary is this amazing...

1676
01:28:57.080 --> 01:29:00.209
smart, sweet human being...

1677
01:29:00.360 --> 01:29:02.442
then I must be
doing something right.

1678
01:29:02.600 --> 01:29:04.648
<b><font color="#808000">(CRYING)</font></b>

1679
01:29:10.880 --> 01:29:13.167
- You're smiling.
- <b><font color="#808000">(LAUGHS TEARFULLY)</font></b>

1680
01:29:24.920 --> 01:29:26.649
- Roberta!
- <b><font color="#808000">(ROBERTA CHUCKLES)</font></b>

1681
01:29:27.560 --> 01:29:28.607
Oh, baby.

1682
01:29:28.680 --> 01:29:30.603
Of all the stunts
you could pull.

1683
01:29:31.560 --> 01:29:33.130
- Where are they going?
- Nowhere.

1684
01:29:33.280 --> 01:29:34.361
Not yet.

1685
01:29:35.440 --> 01:29:36.771
You two, out!

1686
01:29:43.160 --> 01:29:44.576
- What is this?
- Do you want to know...

1687
01:29:44.600 --> 01:29:45.696
how Diane told me she finished?

1688
01:29:45.720 --> 01:29:47.722
What is this unconscionable lie?

1689
01:29:47.880 --> 01:29:49.120
I walked into her apartment

1690
01:29:49.200 --> 01:29:50.296
and she was sitting
on the floor...

1691
01:29:50.320 --> 01:29:51.367
with that baby in her arms

1692
01:29:51.440 --> 01:29:52.576
and you know
what she said to me?

1693
01:29:52.600 --> 01:29:54.090
Admit it. You made this up.

1694
01:29:54.280 --> 01:29:55.770
- She said, "What do I do now?"
- No.

1695
01:29:55.920 --> 01:29:57.285
It is not a completed proof...

1696
01:29:57.440 --> 01:30:00.091
- It is.
- No! It isn't! It can't be.

1697
01:30:00.240 --> 01:30:01.480
Evelyn, stop!

1698
01:30:03.040 --> 01:30:04.530
I'm taking Mary
and I'm raising her

1699
01:30:04.600 --> 01:30:06.125
how I believe Diane
would've wanted.

1700
01:30:06.280 --> 01:30:07.496
- We'll see about that later.
- I realize...

1701
01:30:07.520 --> 01:30:08.851
she's not normal.

1702
01:30:08.920 --> 01:30:10.968
But if Einstein can
ride a bike, so can she.

1703
01:30:11.120 --> 01:30:13.600
Let's just say Diane didn't
tell me, which is nonsense...

1704
01:30:13.800 --> 01:30:15.006
it's a Millennium Problem.

1705
01:30:15.080 --> 01:30:16.923
She would have shared it
with the world.

1706
01:30:17.080 --> 01:30:18.844
That's where
your charade falls apart.

1707
01:30:19.000 --> 01:30:20.968
If she had completed the proof,

1708
01:30:21.040 --> 01:30:22.451
she would have published it.

1709
01:30:25.480 --> 01:30:27.767
Diane instructed me
very clearly.

1710
01:30:30.400 --> 01:30:32.368
That I was only to publish it
postmortem.

1711
01:30:32.960 --> 01:30:34.610
She died six years ago.

1712
01:30:37.560 --> 01:30:39.483
It wasn't her death
she was talking about.

1713
01:30:59.960 --> 01:31:02.167
I tried to talk her out of it.

1714
01:31:03.720 --> 01:31:05.131
But you know how Diane could be

1715
01:31:05.200 --> 01:31:07.043
when she made her mind up
about something.

1716
01:31:08.720 --> 01:31:10.449
I called MIT.

1717
01:31:10.520 --> 01:31:11.601
Shankland's out of his mind

1718
01:31:11.680 --> 01:31:13.682
about the possibility
of publishing it with you.

1719
01:31:15.080 --> 01:31:16.525
He's waiting for your call.

1720
01:31:17.440 --> 01:31:19.536
You're gonna be spending the
next few years of your life...

1721
01:31:19.560 --> 01:31:21.005
defending it.

1722
01:31:21.440 --> 01:31:22.965
You won't have time for her.

1723
01:31:29.280 --> 01:31:30.964
What if I say no?

1724
01:31:37.480 --> 01:31:39.244
Back to Plan A.

1725
01:31:43.240 --> 01:31:45.607
Wait for me to die?

1726
01:31:50.200 --> 01:31:53.329
I know Diane was hard.

1727
01:31:56.560 --> 01:31:57.925
I know she was angry.

1728
01:31:59.520 --> 01:32:02.046
But something really good
came out of this, Evelyn.

1729
01:32:05.880 --> 01:32:07.564
She needs you now.

1730
01:32:08.440 --> 01:32:10.522
You're the best woman
for the job.

1731
01:32:11.120 --> 01:32:12.531
Take it.

1732
01:32:17.640 --> 01:32:21.326
It doesn't seem as though
Diane wanted me to have it.

1733
01:32:25.560 --> 01:32:27.688
Diane didn't always
think things through.

1734
01:32:32.640 --> 01:32:33.846
I'll be outside.

1735
01:32:35.000 --> 01:32:36.331
